= 1H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

Put $e=(N-1)/2$. The odd integer $N$ is an <Euler pseudoprime> to the <coprime> base $b$ when
$$
b^e\equiv\left(\frac bN\right)\pmod N,
$$
where $(b/N)$ is the <Jacobi symbol>.

Let $G=(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z)^\times$ be the <unit group> of <residue classes> modulo $N$, and partition it into the set $E$ of bases satisfying the displayed congruence and the set $W$ of <Euler witness>[Euler witnesses] that do not. If $b_0\in W$, multiplication by $b_0$ maps $E$ <injective function>[injectively] into $W$: indeed, if $b\in E$ and $bb_0$ also lay in $E$, then the <multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol> would give
$$
b^eb_0^e\equiv\left(\frac bN\right)\left(\frac {b_0}N\right)\pmod N.
$$
Cancelling the invertible congruence for $b$ would say that $b_0\in E$, a contradiction. Hence $|W|\geq |E|$, so at least half of the <coprime> bases are witnesses.

It remains to construct one witness for every odd <composite number> $N$. If $N$ is not <squarefree integer>[squarefree], choose an odd <prime number> $p$ with $p^2\mid N$. The <Chinese remainder theorem> gives a unit $b$ such that $b\equiv1+p\pmod {p^a}$ for the full power $p^a\mid N$ and $b\equiv1$ modulo every other prime-power factor. The <binomial theorem> gives
$$
b^e\equiv(1+p)^e\equiv1+ep\pmod {p^2}.
$$
Since $p\nmid e$, this is neither $1$ nor $-1$ modulo $p^2$, whereas the <Jacobi symbol> is always $1$ or $-1$. Thus $b$ is a witness.

If $N$ is squarefree and composite, choose distinct primes $p,q\mid N$. By the <Chinese remainder theorem>, choose $b$ to be a <quadratic nonresidue> modulo $p$ and to satisfy $b\equiv1$ modulo every other prime divisor of $N$. Then $(b/N)=-1$, while $b^e\equiv1\pmod q$, so the Euler congruence again fails. This proves the <existence of an Euler witness for every odd composite integer>.

= 2H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=2H}

The <polynomial Runge theorem> says that if $K\subset\mathbb C$ is <compact set>[compact], $\mathbb C\setminus K$ is <connected space>[connected], and $f$ is <holomorphic function>[holomorphic] on a neighbourhood of $K$, then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is a <polynomial> $p$ such that $\sup_{z\in K}|p(z)-f(z)|<\varepsilon$.

For an explicit approximation on the left semicircle $K=\{z:|z|=1,\operatorname{Re}z\leq0\}$, define
$$
p_n(z)=-\frac14\sum_{j=0}^n\sum_{k=0}^{n^2}\binom{j+k}{k}\left(\frac z4\right)^k.
$$
The two <power series>[power-series expansions]
$$
\frac1z=\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-4)^j}{(z-4)^{j+1}},
\qquad
\frac1{(z-4)^{j+1}}=\frac1{(-4)^{j+1}}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{j+k}{k}\left(\frac z4\right)^k
$$
produce this formula. The first is a <geometric series> whose ratio has modulus at most $4/\sqrt{17}<1$ on $K$. The second converges uniformly on the whole <unit circle>; the elementary bound $\binom{j+k}{k}\leq2^{j+k}$ shows that truncating it at $k=n^2$ gives a total error tending to zero even after summing over $0\leq j\leq n$. Consequently $p_n\to1/z$ <uniform convergence>[uniformly] on $K$. This is an <explicit polynomial approximation of the reciprocal on the left semicircle>.

No such sequence exists on the punctured unit circle $S=\{z:|z|=1,z\ne1\}$. If polynomials $p_n$ converged uniformly there, they would be <uniformly Cauchy>. Continuity gives
$$
\sup_{|z|=1}|p_n(z)-p_m(z)|=\sup_{z\in S}|p_n(z)-p_m(z)|,
$$
so they would converge uniformly on the entire unit circle. The <uniform limit theorem> would force the value at the missing point to be $1$, and hence the limit would be $1/z$ on the full circle. But the <Cauchy integral theorem> gives $\oint p_n(z)\,dz=0$ for every $n$, while <uniform convergence and contour integration> would imply
$$
0=\lim_{n\to\infty}\oint p_n(z)\,dz=\oint\frac{dz}{z}=2\pi i,
$$
a contradiction. This is the <polynomial approximation obstruction on a punctured circle>.

= 3I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=3I}

Use <Shamir's secret sharing> over the <finite field> $\mathbb F_p$. The Chair independently chooses $a_1,\ldots,a_{r-1}$ from the <uniform distribution on a finite set> $\mathbb F_p$ and forms the random <polynomial>
$$
f(X)=N+a_1X+\cdots+a_{r-1}X^{r-1}\in\mathbb F_p[X].
$$
Choose distinct nonzero $x_1,\ldots,x_s\in\mathbb F_p$, with $s$ equal to the number of discs to be issued, and inscribe $(x_i,f(x_i))$ on disc $i$.

Any $r$ discs give $r$ distinct values of a polynomial of degree at most $r-1$. Their <Vandermonde determinant> is nonzero, so <polynomial interpolation> recovers $f$ uniquely and hence recovers the secret as $N=f(0)$.

Conversely, fix any $r-1$ observed discs and any candidate secret $n\in\mathbb F_p$. Interpolating through those $r-1$ points and $(0,n)$ gives exactly one polynomial of degree at most $r-1$. Thus every candidate $n$ is compatible with the observed discs in exactly the same number of ways. Because the coefficients were chosen <uniform distribution on a finite set>[uniformly], the <conditional distribution> of the secret is unchanged after seeing any $r-1$ discs. This proves the <perfect secrecy of Shamir's threshold scheme>.

= 4F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= i
{parent=4f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <context-free grammar> is a quadruple $G=(V,\Sigma,P,S)$ consisting of a finite set $V$ of <nonterminal symbol>[nonterminal symbols], a disjoint finite <alphabet> $\Sigma$ of <terminal symbol>[terminal symbols], a start symbol $S\in V$, and a finite set $P$ of <production rule>[productions] $A\to\alpha$ with $A\in V$ and $\alpha\in(V\cup\Sigma)^*$. A sentence is a terminal <word over an alphabet> $w\in\Sigma^*$ with $S\Rightarrow^*w$, and the generated <formal language> is $\mathcal L(G)=\{w\in\Sigma^*:S\Rightarrow^*w\}$.

The first language is <context-free language>[context-free]. For example, the productions
$$
S\to aaSbb\mid\varepsilon
$$
generate exactly $\{a^{2m}b^{2m}:m\geq0\}$: each recursive production adds two $a$s to the left and two $b$s to the right.

= ii
{parent=4f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The language $L=\{a^{m^2}b^{m^2}:m\geq0\}$ is not <context-free language>[context-free]. Suppose it had <pumping length> $p$ in the <pumping lemma for context-free languages>, and apply the lemma to $w=a^{p^2}b^{p^2}$. Write $w=uvxyz$ with $|vxy|\leq p$, $|vy|>0$, and $uv^ixy^iz\in L$ for every $i\geq0$.

Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be the respective numbers of $a$s and $b$s in $vy$. If $\alpha\ne\beta$, pumping with $i=0$ or $i=2$ destroys equality of the two block lengths. Hence a valid decomposition would require $\alpha=\beta=d>0$. Pumping with $i=2$ would then produce $a^{p^2+d}b^{p^2+d}$, where $1\leq d\leq p$. But
$$
p^2<p^2+d<(p+1)^2,
$$
so $p^2+d$ is not a <square number>. This contradiction proves the <square-count language is not context-free>.

= 5J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= a
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Conditional independence makes the <log-likelihood>
$$
\ell(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^n\log f_{x_i}(Y_i;\theta).
$$
A <maximum-likelihood estimator> is any $\widehat\theta\in\operatorname*{argmax}_{\theta\in\Theta}\ell(\theta)$. If the model has $q$ fitted real parameters, its <Akaike information criterion> is
$$
\operatorname{AIC}=-2\ell(\widehat\theta)+2q.
$$

= b
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the <normal linear model>, the <Gaussian likelihood> gives
$$
\ell(\beta,\sigma^2)
=-\frac n2\log(2\pi\sigma^2)
-\frac1{2\sigma^2}\|Y-X\beta\|^2.
$$
Maximization over $\beta$ gives the <ordinary least squares> estimator $\widehat\beta$, and maximization over the variance gives
$$
\widehat\sigma^2=\frac1n\|Y-X\widehat\beta\|^2.
$$
At this value the quadratic term in $-2\ell$ is $n$. There are $p$ <regression coefficient>[regression coefficients] and one variance parameter, so substitution into the <Akaike information criterion> yields
$$
\operatorname{AIC}=n\bigl(1+\log(2\pi\widehat\sigma^2)\bigr)+2(p+1).
$$

= c
{parent=5j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <hat matrix> $P$ is an <orthogonal projection> of rank $p$, so $I-P$ is an orthogonal projection of rank $n-p$. Because $\varepsilon/\sigma$ is a standard <multivariate normal distribution> vector, <Cochran's theorem> gives
$$
\frac{n\widehat\sigma^2}{\sigma^2}
=\frac{\|(I-P)\varepsilon\|^2}{\sigma^2}
\sim\chi^2_{n-p}.
$$
Thus $\widehat\sigma^2\overset d=(\sigma^2/n)\chi^2_{n-p}$. Substituting this into the preceding formula gives the <distribution of the Akaike information criterion in a normal linear model>:
$$
\operatorname{AIC}\overset d=
n\log(\chi^2_{n-p})
+n\bigl(\log(2\pi\sigma^2/n)+1\bigr)+2(p+1).
$$

= 6B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= i
{parent=6b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Adding the three equations of the <SIR model with waning immunity> gives
$$
\frac{d}{dt}(S+I+R)=0.
$$
Hence <conservation of population> holds: $N=S+I+R$ is an <integral of motion>.

= ii
{parent=6b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At an <endemic equilibrium>, $I^*>0$, so $0=\dot I=I^*(\beta S^*-\gamma)$ forces
$$
S^*=\frac\gamma\beta.
$$
The equation $\dot R=0$ gives $R^*=\gamma I^*/\alpha$. The <conservation law> $S^*+I^*+R^*=N$ therefore yields
$$
I^*=\frac{\alpha(\beta N-\gamma)}{\beta(\alpha+\gamma)},
\qquad
R^*=\frac{\gamma(\beta N-\gamma)}{\beta(\alpha+\gamma)}.
$$
A positive endemic state exists exactly when $\beta N>\gamma$, equivalently when the <basic reproduction number> $\mathcal R_0=\beta N/\gamma$ exceeds one.

= iii
{parent=6b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Eliminate $R=N-S-I$ to obtain the <autonomous differential equation>[two-dimensional autonomous differential equation]
$$
\dot S=\alpha(N-S-I)-\beta SI,
\qquad
\dot I=\beta SI-\gamma I.
$$
Its <Jacobian matrix> at the endemic equilibrium is
$$
J_*=
\begin{pmatrix}
-\alpha-\beta I^*&-\alpha-\gamma\\
\beta I^*&0
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Since $I^*>0$,
$$
\operatorname{tr}J_*=-(\alpha+\beta I^*)<0,
\qquad
\det J_*=\beta I^*(\alpha+\gamma)>0.
$$
The <trace-determinant stability criterion> shows that both <eigenvalue>[eigenvalues] have negative real part. The <endemic equilibrium of the SIR model with waning immunity> is therefore locally <asymptotic stability>[asymptotically stable].

= 7E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= i
{parent=7e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For each nonzero <period lattice> point $\omega$, the <binomial series> gives
$$
\frac1{(z-\omega)^2}-\frac1{\omega^2}
=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}(k+1)\frac{z^k}{\omega^{k+2}}.
$$
Pairing $\omega$ with $-\omega$ cancels every odd power. Hence the <Laurent series> of the <Weierstrass elliptic function> is
$$
\wp(z)=\frac1{z^2}
+3z^2\sum_{\omega\ne0}\omega^{-4}
+5z^4\sum_{\omega\ne0}\omega^{-6}+O(z^6)
=\frac1{z^2}+\frac{g_2}{20}z^2+\frac{g_3}{28}z^4+O(z^6),
$$
which is the stated <Laurent coefficients of the Weierstrass elliptic function>[coefficient formula].

= ii
{parent=7e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set
$$
F(z)=\wp'(z)^2-4\wp(z)^3+g_2\wp(z)+g_3.
$$
This is an <elliptic function>. Write $a=g_2/20$ and $b=g_3/28$ in the expansion from part (i). At a lattice point, the coefficient of $z^{-2}$ in $F$ is $g_2-20a=0$, and its constant coefficient is $g_3-28b=0$; there are no other singular terms. Every apparent pole is therefore a <removable singularity>, and $F$ extends to an entire elliptic function with value zero at the lattice points.

An entire elliptic function is bounded on a <fundamental parallelogram> and hence everywhere. By <Liouville theorem> it is constant, and its value at zero is zero. Thus $F\equiv0$, proving the <Weierstrass elliptic differential equation>
$$
\wp'(z)^2=4\wp(z)^3-g_2\wp(z)-g_3.
$$

= 8B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=8B}

The repulsive force is $\mathbf F=k\mathbf r/r^3=-\nabla(k/r)$, so the <Hamiltonian> is
$$
H=\frac{\mathbf p^2}{2m}+\frac kr.
$$
The <Hamilton's equations> give $\dot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf p/m$ and $\dot{\mathbf p}=k\mathbf r/r^3$. Consequently
$$
\dot{\mathbf L}
=\dot{\mathbf r}\times\mathbf p+\mathbf r\times\dot{\mathbf p}=\mathbf0,
$$
so the <angular momentum> is conserved. Moreover,
$$
\dot{\mathbf p}\times\mathbf L
=k\left(\frac{\mathbf r(\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf p)}{r^3}-\frac{\mathbf p}{r}\right)
=-mk\frac{d\widehat{\mathbf r}}{dt}.
$$
It follows that the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> $\mathbf A=\mathbf p\times\mathbf L+mk\widehat{\mathbf r}$ is conserved. By the <Hamiltonian conservation law>, these time derivatives are exactly $\{\mathbf L,H\}$ and $\{\mathbf A,H\}$, so both <Poisson bracket>[Poisson brackets] vanish.

The <integrals of motion> are the energy $H$, the components of $\mathbf L$, and the components of $\mathbf A$, subject to
$$
\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf L=0,
\qquad
A^2=m^2k^2+2mHL^2.
$$
Taking the <dot product> of $\mathbf A$ with $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives
$$
A\cos\theta=\frac{L^2}{r}+mk.
$$
Therefore the <polar equation of a repulsive inverse-square orbit> is
$$
r=\frac{L^2/(mk)}{(A/(mk))\cos\theta-1}
=\frac{\lambda}{e\cos\theta-1},
$$
where $\lambda=L^2/(mk)\geq0$ and $e=A/(mk)\geq0$. For a nonradial physical scattering orbit $H>0$, the identity above gives $e>1$, as expected for a <hyperbolic Kepler orbit>.

= 9D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=9D}

For equal internal degeneracy, the <number density> of an isotropic ultrarelativistic species is proportional to
$$
\int_0^\infty\frac{p^2\,dp}{e^{\beta pc}\mp1}.
$$
With $x=\beta pc$, compare the <Fermi-Dirac distribution> and <Bose-Einstein distribution> using
$$
\frac1{e^x+1}=\frac1{e^x-1}-\frac2{e^{2x}-1}.
$$
For $s>0$, a <change of variables> in the second term gives the <fermion-to-boson thermal integral ratio>
$$
\int_0^\infty\frac{x^s\,dx}{e^x+1}
=\left(1-2^{-s}\right)
\int_0^\infty\frac{x^s\,dx}{e^x-1}.
$$
Particle number has $s=2$, so $n_f=(1-2^{-2})n_b=3n_b/4$. The <energy density> contains one further factor $E=pc$, so it has $s=3$ and
$$
\rho_f=(1-2^{-3})\rho_b=\frac78\rho_b.
$$

= 10C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Information and Computation}

= i
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <selective phase rotation> $R_g^\phi$ multiplies the component parallel to $|g\rangle$ by $e^{i\phi}$ and fixes its orthogonal complement. Therefore
$$
R_g^\phi U|00\rangle
=e^{i\phi}\sqrt p\,|g\rangle+\sqrt{1-p}\,|b\rangle.
$$

= ii
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Conjugating the <reflection operator> by a <unitary operator> reflects about the conjugated state:
$$
UR_{00}^\pi U^\dagger
=I-2U|00\rangle\langle00|U^\dagger
=I-2|\psi_{\rm in}\rangle\langle\psi_{\rm in}|
=R_{\psi_{\rm in}}^\pi.
$$
Thus one may take $|\psi\rangle=|\psi_{\rm in}\rangle=U|00\rangle$.

= iii
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $p=1/4$, put $|\psi_{\rm in}\rangle=\tfrac12|g\rangle+\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2}|b\rangle$. After the first phase rotation the state is
$$
|v\rangle=\frac{e^{i\phi}}2|g\rangle+\frac{\sqrt3}2|b\rangle,
\qquad
\langle\psi_{\rm in}|v\rangle=\frac{e^{i\phi}+3}{4}.
$$
Since $UR_{00}^\pi U^\dagger=I-2|\psi_{\rm in}\rangle\langle\psi_{\rm in}|$, the requested state is
$$
\frac{e^{i\phi}-3}{4}|g\rangle
-\frac{\sqrt3(e^{i\phi}+1)}4|b\rangle.
$$
It is proportional to $|g\rangle$ exactly when $e^{i\phi}=-1$, hence when $\phi=\pi$. The state is then $-|g\rangle$, whose <global phase> has no physical effect.

= iv
{parent=10c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Choose $U$ to prepare the uniform superposition, so the marked-state probability is $p=1/4$. Apply the <marked-state phase oracle> $R_g^\pi$, followed by the reflection $UR_{00}^\pi U^\dagger$ about the prepared state. Part (iii) shows that this single <Grover search algorithm>[Grover iteration] produces $-|g\rangle$. A <projective measurement> in the four-item basis therefore returns the marked element with probability one. This is <exact Grover search on four entries>.

= 11H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= i
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an odd <prime number> $p$, the <Legendre symbol> is
$$
\left(\frac xp\right)=
\begin{cases}
0,&p\mid x,\\
1,&x\not\equiv0\pmod p\text{ is a <quadratic residue>},\\
-1,&x\text{ is a <quadratic nonresidue>}.
\end{cases}
$$
If $p\nmid x$, <multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol> gives
$$
\left(\frac xp\right)\left(\frac{x^{-1}}p\right)
=\left(\frac1p\right)=1.
$$
Both factors belong to $\{1,-1\}$, so they are equal. Thus $(x^{-1}/p)=(x/p)$.

= ii
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Gauss lemma> states that if $p\nmid a$ and $m$ of the least positive residues of
$$
a,2a,\ldots,\frac{p-1}{2}a
$$
exceed $p/2$, then $(a/p)=(-1)^m$.

To prove it, replace each residue exceeding $p/2$ by its negative, obtaining numbers $r_1,\ldots,r_{(p-1)/2}$ in $\{1,\ldots,(p-1)/2\}$. No two of them are equal modulo $p$: such an equality would give $ia\equiv\pm ja\pmod p$, hence $i=j$ in the allowed range. They therefore permute $1,\ldots,(p-1)/2$, and multiplication gives
$$
a^{(p-1)/2}\left(\frac{p-1}{2}\right)!
\equiv(-1)^m\left(\frac{p-1}{2}\right)!\pmod p.
$$
Cancel the nonzero factorial and apply <Euler's criterion> to obtain the lemma.

For $a=-1$, all $(p-1)/2$ least positive residues $p-1,p-2,\ldots,(p+1)/2$ exceed $p/2$. Hence the <first supplementary law for quadratic reciprocity> is
$$
\left(\frac{-1}{p}\right)=(-1)^{(p-1)/2}.
$$

= iii
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $\chi(x)=(x/p)$. Choose a <quadratic nonresidue> $a$. Multiplication by $a$ permutes $\mathbb F_p$, while <multiplicativity of the Legendre symbol> gives $\chi(ax)=-\chi(x)$. Therefore
$$
\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p}\chi(x)
=\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p}\chi(ax)
=-\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p}\chi(x),
$$
so the <complete Legendre-symbol sum> is zero.

Since $4x(x+1)=(2x+1)^2-1$ and $\chi(4)=1$, the bijection $x\mapsto2x+1$ reduces the next sum to $\sum_y\chi(y^2-1)$. Count pairs $(y,t)\in\mathbb F_p^2$ satisfying $t^2=y^2-1$. On one hand their number is
$$
\sum_y\bigl(1+\chi(y^2-1)\bigr).
$$
On the other hand $(y-t)(y+t)=1$, and every $u\in\mathbb F_p^\times$ gives exactly one pair by $y-t=u$, $y+t=u^{-1}$. There are therefore $p-1$ pairs, proving the <quadratic Legendre-symbol correlation>
$$
\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p}\chi(x(x+1))=-1.
$$

For $1\leq z\leq p-2$, the indicator that both $z$ and $z+1$ are quadratic residues is $(1+\chi(z))(1+\chi(z+1))/4$. Summing and using the two identities above gives the <number of consecutive nonzero quadratic residues modulo an odd prime>
$$
\frac14\left(p-2-\chi(-1)-1-1\right)
=\boxed{\frac{p-4-(-1)^{(p-1)/2}}4}.
$$

= 12F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= Solution
{parent=12F}

A <deterministic finite automaton> is a tuple $(Q,\Sigma,\delta,q_0,F)$ with finite state set $Q$, finite input alphabet $\Sigma$, transition function $\delta:Q\times\Sigma\to Q$, initial state $q_0$, and accepting-state set $F\subseteq Q$. It accepts a word when the <extended transition function of a deterministic finite automaton> carries $q_0$ to a state in $F$. A <regular language> is a language accepted by some deterministic finite automaton.

The <pumping lemma for regular languages> says that if a regular language is accepted by an automaton with $N$ states, every accepted word $w$ of length at least $N$ has a decomposition $w=xyz$ with $|xy|\leq N$, $|y|>0$, and $xy^iz$ in the language for every $i\geq0$. Indeed, among the $N+1$ states visited before and after the first $N$ symbols, two are equal by the <pigeonhole principle>. The intervening nonempty word $y$ labels a loop, which may be traversed any number of times without changing the final accepting state.

In <binary numeral system>[base two], the powers of two have representations $10^n$, so their language is the <regular expression> $10^*$ and is regular.

Suppose instead that their <decimal representation>[base-ten representations] formed a regular language. Apply the pumping lemma to a sufficiently long decimal power of two $xyz$, and put $d=|y|>0$. Pumping gives decimal integers $a_i$ represented by $xy^iz$, all powers of two. A direct place-value calculation shows that
$$
a_{i+1}-10^d a_i=C
$$
for a constant $C$ independent of $i$. The lengths, and hence the exponents in $a_i=2^{n_i}$, tend to infinity. Dividing by $a_i$ gives
$$
2^{n_{i+1}-n_i}-10^d=\frac C{2^{n_i}}.
$$
For large $i$, the right side has absolute value less than one while the left side is an integer, so it must vanish. This would make a power of two equal to $10^d=2^d5^d$, impossible for $d>0$ by <unique prime factorization>. Thus the decimal powers of two are not a <regular language>.

= 13B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= i
{parent=13b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Removing the death term with $u(x,t)=e^{\mu t}n(x,t)$ gives the <heat equation> $u_t=Du_{xx}$ with initial data $N\delta(x)$. The <heat kernel> therefore yields
$$
n(x,t)=\frac{N}{\sqrt{4\pi Dt}}
\exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}-\mu t\right).
$$
For each fixed $x$, the exponential mortality factor and diffusive prefactor show that $n(x,t)\to0$ as $t\to\infty$. Its spatial integral is $Ne^{-\mu t}$, the expected number of surviving larvae.

= ii
{parent=13b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Because $e(x,0)=0$, the egg density is the accumulated deposition
$$
e(x,t)=\lambda\int_0^t n(x,s)\,ds.
$$
It is convenient to use the <Fourier transform>. The larval equation gives
$$
\widehat n(k,t)=N e^{-(Dk^2+\mu)t},
\qquad
\widehat e(k,\infty)=\frac{N\lambda}{Dk^2+\mu}.
$$
Applying the stated inverse-transform integral with $\alpha=\sqrt{\mu/D}$ gives the <remnant density from diffusion with mortality and deposition>
$$
e(x,\infty)=\frac{N\lambda}{2\sqrt{D\mu}}
\exp\left(-|x|\sqrt{\frac\mu D}\right).
$$

= iii
{parent=13b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Each larva undergoes <diffusion equation>[diffusion] over its exponentially distributed lifetime of mean $1/\mu$ and deposits eggs at rate $\lambda$ wherever it is found. The characteristic <diffusion length> before death is $\sqrt{D/\mu}$, which explains the exponential spatial decay. Integrating the remnant over space gives
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e(x,\infty)\,dx=\frac{N\lambda}{\mu}.
$$
This is also immediate physically: each of the $N$ larvae lives for mean time $1/\mu$ and therefore lays an expected $\lambda/\mu$ eggs. The eggs remain as a stationary record because they neither die nor diffuse.

= 14D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= i
{parent=14d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Expand the <density contrast> in <Fourier mode>[Fourier modes], $\delta(\mathbf x,t)=\int\delta_{\mathbf k}(t)e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x}\,d^3k$. In the static universe each mode satisfies
$$
\ddot\delta_{\mathbf k}+c_s^2(k^2-k_J^2)\delta_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$
For $k>k_J$ there are <acoustic wave>[sound waves]
$$
\delta_{\mathbf k}=A_{\mathbf k}e^{i\omega_k t}+B_{\mathbf k}e^{-i\omega_k t},
\qquad
\omega_k=c_s\sqrt{k^2-k_J^2}.
$$
For $k<k_J$, <Jeans instability> gives growing and decaying exponentials with rate $c_s\sqrt{k_J^2-k^2}$. At the <Jeans wavenumber> the two solutions are constant and linear in time.

= ii
{parent=14d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On long wavelengths the gradient term is negligible. The <Friedmann equation> implies
$$
c_s^2k_J^2=\frac{4\pi G\bar\rho}{c^2}=\frac32H^2.
$$
During <matter domination>, $a\propto t^{2/3}$ and $H=2/(3t)$, so the <linear growth equation> becomes
$$
\ddot\delta+\frac4{3t}\dot\delta-\frac2{3t^2}\delta=0.
$$
The <power-law ansatz> $\delta\propto t^s$ gives $s=2/3$ or $s=-1$. Since $a\propto t^{2/3}$, the growing and decaying modes are
$$
\boxed{\delta_+\propto a,\qquad \delta_-\propto a^{-3/2}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=14d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Under the stated approximation the perturbation equation is
$$
\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta=0,
\qquad
\dot\delta\propto a^{-2}.
$$
During <radiation domination>, $a\propto t^{1/2}$, so $\dot\delta\propto t^{-1}$ and
$$
\delta=C_1+C_2\log t=C_1+2C_2\log a.
$$
Matter perturbations therefore have only <logarithmic growth of matter perturbations during radiation domination>.

In a <curvature-dominated universe>, $a\propto t$, so $\dot\delta\propto t^{-2}$ and
$$
\delta=C_1+C_2t^{-1}=C_1+C_2a^{-1}.
$$
The nondecaying mode is constant: curvature domination gives <freezing of matter perturbations during curvature domination>, rather than continued growth.

= 15C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Information and Computation}

= a
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The promise is <Simon's problem>. If $z=0^n$, then $f(x)=f(y)$ implies $x=y$, so $f$ is an <injective function>. If $z\ne0^n$, then each value has exactly the two preimages $x$ and $x\mathbin\oplus z$; thus $f$ is <two-to-one function>[two-to-one] and is constant on the two-element cosets of $\{0^n,z\}\leq(\mathbb Z_2)^n$.

= b
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Starting from $|0^n\rangle|0^n\rangle$, the first layer of <Hadamard gate>[Hadamard gates] gives
$$
2^{-n/2}\sum_{x\in B_n}|x\rangle|0^n\rangle.
$$
The <Boolean quantum oracle> then gives
$$
2^{-n/2}\sum_{x\in B_n}|x\rangle|f(x)\rangle.
$$
Using the <Walsh-Hadamard transform> identity
$$
H^{\otimes n}|x\rangle
=2^{-n/2}\sum_{y\in B_n}(-1)^{x\cdot y}|y\rangle,
$$
the final state is
$$
2^{-n}\sum_{x,y\in B_n}(-1)^{x\cdot y}|y\rangle|f(x)\rangle.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

When $z=0^n$, the function $f$ is injective, so the states $|f(x)\rangle$ are distinct <computational basis> states and hence form an <orthonormal set>. Therefore, for arbitrary signs $\varphi(x,y)$,
$$
\left\|\sum_x\varphi(x,y)|f(x)\rangle\right\|^2
=\sum_x|\varphi(x,y)|^2
=2^n
=\left\|\sum_x\varphi(x,y)|x\rangle\right\|^2.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In the final state, the second-register amplitude vector associated with outcome $y$ is
$$
2^{-n}\sum_x(-1)^{x\cdot y}|f(x)\rangle.
$$
For injective $f$, part (ii) says that its squared norm is $2^{-2n}2^n=2^{-n}$. By the <Born rule for a product-basis measurement>, every $y\in B_n$ is therefore observed with probability $2^{-n}$: the input register is uniformly distributed.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Now let $z\ne0^n$ and choose one representative $x$ from each pair $\{x,x\mathbin\oplus z\}$. Since $f(x)=f(x\mathbin\oplus z)$ and
$$
(-1)^{(x\mathbin\oplus z)\cdot y}
=(-1)^{x\cdot y}(-1)^{z\cdot y},
$$
the unnormalized second-register amplitude for outcome $y$ is
$$
\sum_{\text{pairs }[x]}(-1)^{x\cdot y}
\bigl(1+(-1)^{z\cdot y}\bigr)|f(x)\rangle.
$$
If $y\cdot z=1$, every paired contribution cancels, so the probability is zero. If $y\cdot z=0$, every coefficient has magnitude two. The $2^{n-1}$ distinct pair values give orthogonal states, so the squared norm before the overall $2^{-n}$ factor is $4\cdot2^{n-1}=2^{n+1}$. The probability is therefore
$$
2^{-2n}2^{n+1}=2^{-(n-1)}.
$$
Thus one run samples uniformly from the $2^{n-1}$ strings in the <orthogonal complement over the binary field> $\{y:y\cdot z=0\}$, which is the sampling step in <Simon's algorithm>.

= 16H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=16H}

A <class in set theory> in the model $V$ is a collection
$$
C=\{x\in V:V\models\varphi(x,a_1,\ldots,a_k)\}
$$
defined by a first-order formula with parameters $a_i\in V$. A <set-theoretic class function> is a definable class relation $F$ for which every input in its domain has exactly one output. Informally, the <axiom schema of replacement> says that the image $F``a$ of any set $a$ under any such function class is again a set.

Define the class function $F:\omega\to V$ by the <natural-number recursion theorem>,
$$
F(0)=\omega,
\qquad
F(n+1)=\mathcal P(F(n)).
$$
Then $F(n)=z_n$, and <axiom schema of replacement>[Replacement] applied to the set $\omega$ gives
$$
y=F``\omega=\{z_n:n\in\omega\}
$$
as a set.

Call a set small when it injects into some $z_n$. Every <natural number> $m$ is finite, and every member of $\operatorname{TC}(\{m\})$ is finite, so each injects into $z_0=\omega$. Hence $m\in\mathbf{HS}$. The set $\omega$ itself injects into $z_0$, and its hereditary members are natural numbers, so $\omega\in\mathbf{HS}$.

We next prove $z_n\in\mathbf{HS}$ by <mathematical induction>. The case $z_0=\omega$ was just proved. If $z_n\in\mathbf{HS}$ and $u\in z_{n+1}=\mathcal P(z_n)$, then $u\subseteq z_n$, so inclusion injects $u$ into $z_n$ and makes $u$ small. Every set below $u$ in its <transitive closure> is already below $z_n$ and is small by the induction hypothesis. The set $z_{n+1}$ itself injects into $z_{n+1}$. Thus every member of $\operatorname{TC}(\{z_{n+1}\})$ is small.

By <Cantor theorem>, $|z_{n+1}|>|z_n|$, so the $z_n$ are distinct and $z_n\mapsto n$ injects $y$ into $\omega=z_0$. Thus $y$ is small. Every other member of $\operatorname{TC}(\{y\})$ belongs to $\operatorname{TC}(\{z_n\})$ for some $n$, and is small by the preceding paragraph. Therefore $y\in\mathbf{HS}$. This proves the <finite-power-set hereditary-small construction>.

The structure $(\mathbf{HS},\in)$ is not a model of ZF because it fails the <axiom of union>. If $\bigcup y$ were small, it would inject into some $z_m$. But $z_{m+1}\subseteq\bigcup y$, since $z_{m+1}\in y$, so restriction would inject
$$
z_{m+1}=\mathcal P(z_m)\longrightarrow z_m.
$$
Together with the singleton injection $z_m\to\mathcal P(z_m)$, the <Cantor-Schröder-Bernstein theorem> would produce a bijection, contradicting <Cantor theorem>. Hence $\bigcup y$ is not small and therefore does not belong to $\mathbf{HS}$. Since $y\in\mathbf{HS}$ has no union inside the class, the Union axiom fails.

= 17G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= i
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Turan theorem> states that among all $N$-vertex graphs containing no $K_{r+1}$, the maximum number of <edges> is attained by the <Turan graph> $T_r(N)$, the <complete multipartite graph> whose $r$ part sizes differ by at most one.

Here is the <Zykov symmetrization> proof. Start with an extremal $K_{r+1}$-free graph. If nonadjacent vertices $u,v$ have different neighbourhoods, replace the lower-degree vertex by a clone of the higher-degree one: delete all its incident edges and join it to precisely the neighbours of the other vertex. This does not decrease the number of edges and cannot create a $K_{r+1}$, because a clique using the clone corresponds to one using the original vertex. Repeating the operation, with the standard tie-breaking that merges equal-neighbourhood classes, produces a complete multipartite extremal graph. It has at most $r$ nonempty parts, since choosing one vertex from each part gives a clique.

If two part sizes satisfy $a\geq b+2$, moving one vertex from the larger part to the smaller changes the number of cross-edges by
$$
a-b-1>0.
$$
Thus an extremal partition has exactly $r$ parts whose sizes differ by at most one. This is $T_r(N)$ and proves the theorem. For $r=2$ it is <Mantel theorem>: a triangle-free graph on $N$ vertices has at most $\lfloor N^2/4\rfloor$ edges.

= ii
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $|V(G)|=2n$ and $e(G)=n^2+1$. By <Mantel theorem>, a <triangle-free graph> of this order has at most
$$
\left\lfloor\frac{(2n)^2}{4}\right\rfloor=n^2
$$
edges, so $G$ contains a <triangle in a graph>.

If $n=2$, then $G$ has four vertices and five of the six possible edges, so it is $K_4$ with one edge deleted. Exactly the two triangles containing neither both endpoints of the deleted edge remain. Hence $G$ contains two triangles.

= iii
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $t(G)$ be the number of <triangles in a graph>. Count incidences $(e,\Delta)$ in which the edge $e$ lies in the triangle $\Delta$. Every edge contributes at least one incidence by hypothesis, while every triangle contributes exactly three. Therefore
$$
3t(G)\geq e(G)=n^2+1,
\qquad
 t(G)\geq\frac{n^2+1}{3}.
$$
This is the <edge-triangle incidence bound>.

= iv
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

If the edge $uv$ lies in no triangle, no vertex can be adjacent to both endpoints; hence the <vertex neighbourhood>[neighbourhoods] satisfy $\Gamma(u)\cap\Gamma(v)=\varnothing$.

Suppose $|\Gamma(u)|+|\Gamma(v)|=2n$. The two disjoint neighbourhoods then partition $V(G)$. If both were <independent set>[independent], every edge would run between these two parts, so $G$ would be <bipartite graph>[bipartite] and
$$
e(G)\leq|\Gamma(u)|\,|\Gamma(v)|
\leq\left(\frac{|\Gamma(u)|+|\Gamma(v)|}{2}\right)^2=n^2,
$$
contrary to $e(G)=n^2+1$. Thus at least one neighbourhood contains an edge, and that edge forms a triangle with $u$ or $v$.

We now prove by induction on $n\geq2$ the <triangle lower bound one edge above the Mantel threshold>: every graph on $2n$ vertices with at least $n^2+1$ edges has at least $n$ triangles. Part (ii) is the base case. For $n\geq3$, if every edge lies in a triangle, part (iii) gives
$$
t(G)\geq\frac{n^2+1}{3}\geq n.
$$
Otherwise choose an edge $uv$ lying in no triangle and put $s=d(u)+d(v)\leq2n$. Deleting $u,v$ removes $s-1$ edges, so the graph $G'=G-u-v$ has
$$
e(G')=n^2+2-s\geq(n-1)^2+1.
$$
If $s=2n$, induction gives at least $n-1$ triangles in $G'$, while the nonindependence proved above supplies a further triangle through $u$ or $v$.

If $s\leq2n-1$, then $e(G')\geq(n-1)^2+2$. Induction first supplies a triangle $\Delta$ in $G'$. Delete one edge of $\Delta$; the remaining graph still has at least $(n-1)^2+1$ edges, so induction supplies $n-1$ triangles avoiding that edge. Together with $\Delta$, these give at least $n$ distinct triangles in $G$.

= 18G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= a
{parent=18g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because $L/K$ is a finite <Galois extension>,
$$
[L:K]=|\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)|=|A_{10}|=\frac{10!}{2}=1\,814\,400.
$$

Suppose the irreducible quadratic $f$ became reducible over $L$. It would then have a root $\alpha\in L$, and $K(\alpha)/K$ would be a quadratic intermediate extension. By the <fundamental theorem of Galois theory>, $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K(\alpha))$ would be a subgroup of index two in $A_{10}$ and hence a <normal subgroup> by the <index-two subgroup is normal> result. But the <simplicity of alternating groups> says that $A_{10}$ is simple, while this subgroup would be proper and nontrivial. This contradiction proves that $f$ remains irreducible in $L[x]$.

= b
{parent=18g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <eleventh cyclotomic field> $L=\mathbb Q(\xi_{11})$ has
$$
\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)
\cong(\mathbb Z/11\mathbb Z)^\times\cong C_{10}.
$$
The cyclic group $C_{10}$ has exactly one subgroup of each order $1,2,5,10$. The <Galois correspondence> therefore gives exactly four subfields:
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
M&[M:\mathbb Q]&\text{an explicit generator}\\ \hline
\mathbb Q&1&-\\
\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-11})&2&
\eta=\xi_{11}+\xi_{11}^3+\xi_{11}^4+\xi_{11}^5+\xi_{11}^9\\
\mathbb Q(\xi_{11}+\xi_{11}^{-1})&5&
\theta=\xi_{11}+\xi_{11}^{-1}\\
L&10&\xi_{11}.
\end{array}
$$
The <quadratic Gaussian period in the eleventh cyclotomic field> satisfies $\eta=(-1+\sqrt{-11})/2$, while $\theta$ generates the <maximal real subfield of the eleventh cyclotomic field>. Every subgroup of the abelian Galois group is normal, so all four subfields are Galois over $\mathbb Q$.

Each nontrivial proper subfield has prime degree, two or five. If $a\in M\setminus\mathbb Q$, then $\mathbb Q\subsetneq\mathbb Q(a)\subseteq M$, and the tower law forces $\mathbb Q(a)=M$. Thus every such element is a <primitive element of a field extension>[primitive element]. The displayed $\eta$ and $\theta$ are the requested examples. Finally, $\xi_{11}$ itself is a primitive element for $L/\mathbb Q$.

= 19F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= Solution
{parent=19F}

For subgroups $H,K\leq G$ and an $H$-representation $W$, <Mackey restriction formula> is
$$
\operatorname{Res}_K^G\operatorname{Ind}_H^G W
\cong
\bigoplus_{x\in K\backslash G/H}
\operatorname{Ind}_{K\cap xHx^{-1}}^K
\operatorname{Res}_{K\cap xHx^{-1}}^{xHx^{-1}}({}^xW).
$$
<Frobenius reciprocity> states
$$
\langle\operatorname{Ind}_H^G\chi,\psi\rangle_G
=\langle\chi,\operatorname{Res}_H^G\psi\rangle_H.
$$
Applying these with $K=H$ gives
$$
\left\langle\operatorname{Ind}_H^G\chi,
\operatorname{Ind}_H^G\chi\right\rangle_G
=
\sum_{x\in H\backslash G/H}
\left\langle
\operatorname{Res}_{H\cap xHx^{-1}}^H\chi,
\operatorname{Res}_{H\cap xHx^{-1}}^{xHx^{-1}}({}^x\chi)
\right\rangle.
$$
The identity double coset contributes one exactly when $W$ is irreducible, and every summand is a nonnegative integer. This proves <Mackey irreducibility criterion>: the induced representation is irreducible exactly when $W$ is irreducible and every nonidentity double-coset summand vanishes.

Now take $G=S_n$, $H=S_{n-1}$, and $x=(n-1\ n)\notin H$. Then
$$
H\cap xHx^{-1}=S_{n-2},
$$
the subgroup fixing both $n-1$ and $n$. Conjugation by $x$ fixes every element of this subgroup, so the two representations appearing in the corresponding Mackey summand are both $\operatorname{Res}_{S_{n-2}}^{S_{n-1}}W$. Their inner product is positive because this restriction is nonzero. Hence the <Mackey irreducibility criterion> fails whenever $W$ is irreducible. If $W$ is reducible, induction preserves its direct-sum decomposition, so the induced representation is again reducible. Therefore $\operatorname{Ind}_{S_{n-1}}^{S_n}W$ is never irreducible for $n\geq2$.

= 20F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= a
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <geometric realization of a simplicial complex> consists of the filled triangle $\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\rangle$ together with the two extra edges $\langle v_1,v_4\rangle$ and $\langle v_2,v_4\rangle$. Thus the triangle on $v_1,v_2,v_4$ has only its boundary:

\Image[past-exam-of-the-mathematics-course-of-the-university-of-cambridge/2020/ii/paper-3-simplicial-complex.png]

= b
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the orientations
$$
t=[v_1v_2v_3],
\qquad
e_{ij}=[v_iv_j]\quad(i<j).
$$
The <simplicial chain complex> has
$$
C_2\cong\mathbb Z,
\qquad C_1\cong\mathbb Z^5,
\qquad C_0\cong\mathbb Z^4,
$$
with
$$
\partial_2t=e_{23}-e_{13}+e_{12},
\qquad
\partial_1e_{ij}=v_j-v_i.
$$
The map $\partial_2$ is injective, so $H_2(K)=0$. The complex is connected, so $H_0(K)\cong\mathbb Z$ and $\operatorname{rank}\partial_1=3$. Hence $\ker\partial_1$ has rank $5-3=2$; quotienting by the rank-one primitive subgroup $\operatorname{im}\partial_2$ gives
$$
H_1(K)\cong\mathbb Z.
$$
There are no higher simplices. Therefore
$$
H_n(K)\cong
\begin{cases}
\mathbb Z,&n=0,1,\\
0,&n\geq2.
\end{cases}
$$
The result agrees with the <Euler characteristic> $4-5+1=0$.

= c
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Define a <simplicial map> $j:K\to L$ on vertices by
$$
j(v_1)=v_1,qquad j(v_2)=v_2,qquad
j(v_3)=v_1,qquad j(v_4)=v_4.
$$
The image of every simplex is a simplex of $L$, and $j\circ i=\operatorname{id}_L$. The maps $i\circ j$ and $\operatorname{id}_K$ are <contiguous simplicial maps>, since the union of their images on every simplex is contained in a simplex of $K$. Their realizations are therefore homotopic, so $|j|$ is a <homotopy inverse> of $|i|$.

Here is an explicit <chain homotopy>. Put $f=i_\bullet j_\bullet$ and use the orientations from part (b). Define
$$
h_0(v_3)=e_{13},
\qquad h_0(v_1)=h_0(v_2)=h_0(v_4)=0,
$$
$$
h_1(e_{23})=t,
\qquad h_1(e_{12})=h_1(e_{13})=h_1(e_{14})=h_1(e_{24})=0,
\qquad h_2=0.
$$
We verify
$$
\partial h+h\partial=\operatorname{id}-f.
$$
It is immediate on every vertex except $v_3$, where $\partial h_0(v_3)=v_3-v_1=v_3-f(v_3)$. It is immediate on the fixed edges. For the remaining edges,
$$
(\partial h+h\partial)e_{13}=h_0(v_3-v_1)=e_{13}
=e_{13}-f(e_{13}),
$$
$$
(\partial h+h\partial)e_{23}
=\partial t+h_0(v_3-v_2)
=(e_{23}-e_{13}+e_{12})+e_{13}
=e_{23}+e_{12}
=e_{23}-f(e_{23}),
$$
because $f(e_{23})=[v_2v_1]=-e_{12}$. Finally,
$$
(\partial h+h\partial)t=h_1(e_{23}-e_{13}+e_{12})=t=t-f(t),
$$
since $f(t)$ is degenerate and hence zero. Thus $h$ is the required chain homotopy.

= 21I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= a
{parent=21i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a bounded <linear operator> $T:H\to H$, the <spectrum> is
$$
\sigma(T)=\{\lambda\in\mathbb C:T-\lambda I\text{ has no bounded inverse}\},
$$
while the <point spectrum> is the set of <eigenvalue>[eigenvalues], equivalently the $\lambda$ for which $T-\lambda I$ is not injective.

A <compact operator> maps the unit ball to a relatively compact set. A <self-adjoint operator> satisfies $T=T^*$, or $\langle Tx,y\rangle=\langle x,Ty\rangle$ for all $x,y\in H$. A <finite-rank operator> has finite-dimensional image.

= b
{parent=21i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Compactness gives a stronger statement than the one requested. Since $T(B_H)$ has compact closure, finitely many open balls of radius $\delta$ cover it. Choose their centres from $T(B_H)$ and let $E$ be their finite-dimensional linear span. Then
$$
\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}\operatorname{dist}(Tx,E)<\delta.
$$
For the <orthogonal projection> $P_E$, best approximation in a Hilbert space gives
$$
\|(I-P_E)Tx\|<\delta
\qquad(\|x\|\leq1).
$$
In particular this holds for every vector $e_n$ in the given <orthonormal basis>.

The operator $P_ET$ has image in $E$ and hence has <finite rank operator>[finite rank], while
$$
\|T-P_ET\|
=\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}\|(I-P_E)Tx\|<\delta.
$$
Thus every compact operator is an <operator norm> limit of finite-rank operators. This is the <finite-rank approximation theorem for compact operators on a Hilbert space>.

= c
{parent=21i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $E=\operatorname{im}S$, a finite-dimensional subspace, and decompose $H=E\oplus E^\perp$. The restriction
$$
(S-\lambda I)|_E:E\to E
$$
is injective, because a nonzero vector in its kernel would be a $\lambda$-eigenvector of $S$. By finite-dimensionality it is therefore surjective.

Given $y=y_E+y_\perp$, first put $x_\perp=-y_\perp/\lambda$. Then $Sx_\perp\in E$, so choose $x_E\in E$ such that
$$
(S-\lambda I)x_E=y_E-Sx_\perp.
$$
For $x=x_E+x_\perp$ we obtain $(S-\lambda I)x=y$. Hence $S-\lambda I$ is surjective. This is the <finite-rank Fredholm alternative>.

= d
{parent=21i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $A=T-\lambda I$. We first prove that $\operatorname{im}A$ is dense. Otherwise some nonzero $y$ lies in its <orthogonal complement>, so
$$
A^*y=0,
\qquad T^*y=\overline\lambda y.
$$
By part (b), choose finite-dimensional spaces $E_j$ containing $y$ such that $S_j=P_{E_j}T\to T$ in <operator norm>. Since
$$
S_j^*y=T^*P_{E_j}y=T^*y=\overline\lambda y,
$$
the operator $S_j^*-\overline\lambda I$ is not injective. Therefore $S_j-\lambda I$ is not surjective; the contrapositive of part (c) makes $\lambda$ an eigenvalue of $S_j$. Choose unit vectors $x_j$ with $S_jx_j=\lambda x_j$. Then
$$
\|Tx_j-\lambda x_j\|
\leq\|T-S_j\|\longrightarrow0.
$$
Compactness gives a subsequence for which $Tx_j$ converges. Since $\lambda\ne0$, the displayed relation makes $x_j$ converge to a unit vector $x$, and continuity gives $Tx=\lambda x$, contradicting the hypothesis. Thus $\operatorname{im}A$ is dense.

Next suppose $A$ were not bounded below, or equivalently that its <lower norm of an operator>[lower norm] were zero. There would be unit vectors $x_j$ with $Ax_j\to0$, hence
$$
Tx_j=\lambda x_j+o(1).
$$
Again a convergent subsequence of $Tx_j$ forces the corresponding $x_j$ to converge to a unit vector $x$ satisfying $Ax=0$, another contradiction. Therefore some $c>0$ satisfies
$$
\|(T-\lambda I)x\|\geq c\|x\|.
$$

This lower bound makes $\operatorname{im}A$ closed: if $Ax_j$ converges, then $x_j$ is Cauchy and its limit maps to the same limit. Since the image is both dense and closed, it is all of $H$. We have proved the <Fredholm alternative for a compact operator>: every nonzero spectral value of a compact operator is an eigenvalue.

= 22I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis of Functions}

= a
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <continuous dual space> $X'$ consists of all bounded linear maps $\Lambda:X\to\mathbb C$, with
$$
\|\Lambda\|_{X'}
=\sup_{\|x\|_X\leq1}|\Lambda x|.
$$
If $Y=L^p(\mathbb R^n)$ with $1\leq p<\infty$ and $q$ is the <Holder inequality>[conjugate exponent], then <duality of Lp spaces> identifies $Y'$ isometrically with $L^q(\mathbb R^n)$, including $q=\infty$ when $p=1$. Every functional has the form
$$
\Lambda_h(f)=\int_{\mathbb R^n}f(x)\overline{h(x)}\,dx,
\qquad h\in L^q,
$$
and $\|\Lambda_h\|=\|h\|_q$.

= b
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Norm convergence means
$$
\|\Lambda_j-\Lambda\|_{X'}\longrightarrow0.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Weak convergence in $X'$ means
$$
\Phi(\Lambda_j)\longrightarrow\Phi(\Lambda)
\qquad\text{for every }\Phi\in X''.
$$
Norm convergence implies weak convergence because every $\Phi\in X''$ is norm-continuous.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

<Weak-star topology>[Weak-star convergence] means pointwise convergence on the predual:
$$
\Lambda_j(x)\longrightarrow\Lambda(x)
\qquad\text{for every }x\in X.
$$
For each $x\in X$, its image $J_Xx\in X''$ under the <canonical embedding into the bidual> satisfies $(J_Xx)(\Lambda)=\Lambda(x)$. Thus weak convergence implies weak-star convergence, and altogether
$$
\text{norm convergence}\ \Longrightarrow\
\text{weak convergence}\ \Longrightarrow\
\text{weak-star convergence}.
$$

For the requested example, let
$$
f_j(x)=\sin(jx)\in L^\infty(\mathbb R).
$$
The <Riemann-Lebesgue lemma> gives $f_j\mathrel{\stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup}}0$ in $(L^1)'$. Since
$$
f_j(x)^2=\frac12-\frac12\cos(2jx),
$$
the same lemma gives $f_j^2\mathrel{\stackrel{*}{\rightharpoonup}}1/2$. Hence $f=0$ and $g=1/2\ne f^2$, exhibiting the <failure of weak-star convergence to commute with squaring>.

= c
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

On $C_c^0(\mathbb R^n)$ equipped with the $L^\infty$ norm, point evaluation is linear and satisfies
$$
|\Lambda f|=|f(0)|\leq\|f\|_\infty,
$$
with equality for a compactly supported function equal to one near zero. Thus $\|\Lambda\|=1$. The <Hahn-Banach theorem> extends it, without increasing its norm, to a continuous functional $\widetilde\Lambda$ on $L^\infty(\mathbb R^n)$.

This extension cannot be represented by an $L^1$ function. If $h\in L^1$ satisfied $\widetilde\Lambda(f)=\int fh$, choose continuous functions $0\leq f_k\leq1$ with $f_k(0)=1$ and support in the ball of radius $1/k$. Then
$$
1=\widetilde\Lambda(f_k)=\int f_kh\longrightarrow0
$$
by the <absolute continuity of the Lebesgue integral>, a contradiction. Therefore $(L^\infty)'$ strictly contains the functionals represented by $L^1$; this is a <singular functional on L infinity>.

Finally, $L^p(\mathbb R^n)$ is a <reflexive Banach space> exactly when
$$
1<p<\infty.
$$
The endpoint spaces $L^1$ and $L^\infty$ are not reflexive.

= 23F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=23F}

For the <period lattice> $\Lambda=\langle\lambda,\mu\rangle$, define the <Weierstrass elliptic function> by
$$
\wp(z)=\frac1{z^2}
+\sum_{\omega\in\Lambda\setminus\{0\}}
\left(\frac1{(z-\omega)^2}-\frac1{\omega^2}\right).
$$
To prove <normal convergence of the Weierstrass elliptic-function series>, let $K\subset\mathbb C\setminus\Lambda$ be compact and choose $R$ with $|z|\leq R$ on $K$. For $|\omega|>2R$,
$$
\left|\frac1{(z-\omega)^2}-\frac1{\omega^2}\right|
=\left|\frac{2\omega z-z^2}{\omega^2(z-\omega)^2}\right|
\leq\frac{C_K}{|\omega|^3}.
$$
The comparison series $\sum_{\omega\ne0}|\omega|^{-3}$ converges by the stated lattice-sum criterion. The <Weierstrass M-test> gives uniform convergence on $K$; since $K$ was arbitrary, the series converges locally uniformly away from $\Lambda$. The <locally uniform convergence of holomorphic functions> makes its sum holomorphic there, and the displayed principal term gives a double pole at every lattice point.

The function $\wp$ is even and satisfies
$$
\wp(z)=\wp(w)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
z\equiv\pm w\pmod\Lambda.
$$
The three nonzero <two-torsion point of a complex torus>[two-torsion points] $z_1,z_2,z_3$ are distinct modulo $\Lambda$ and each equals its own negative modulo $\Lambda$. Therefore no two can have the same $\wp$-value, so $e_1,e_2,e_3$ are distinct. Equivalently, $\wp$ is a degree-two branched map and each half-lattice point already occupies its fiber with multiplicity two.

For a transitive example, take the <equianharmonic lattice>
$$
\Lambda=\mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\omega,
\qquad \omega=e^{2\pi i/3},
$$
and define $\theta([z])=[\omega z]$. Multiplication by $\omega$ preserves $\Lambda$, so $\theta:\mathbb C/\Lambda\to\mathbb C/\Lambda$ is a <conformal equivalence>. On the nonzero half-lattice points it acts by
$$
\left[\frac12\right]
\longmapsto\left[\frac\omega2\right]
\longmapsto\left[\frac{1+\omega}{2}\right]
\longmapsto\left[\frac12\right],
$$
because $\omega^2=-1-\omega$. Thus it acts transitively.

= 24F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= i
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let the <smooth projective curve> be the <projective completion> of $f(x,y)=0$, of <degree of a polynomial>[degree] $d$, and suppose after interchanging $x$ and $y$ if necessary that $f_y=\partial f/\partial y$ is nonzero. A basis of its <holomorphic differential forms> is
$$
\left\{
x^iy^j\frac{dx}{f_y}:i,j\geq0,\ i+j\leq d-3
\right\}.
$$
Equivalently one may use $-x^iy^jdy/f_x$. These expressions agree on their common domain because differentiating $f(x,y)=0$ gives $f_xdx+f_ydy=0$. The allowed <monomials> are precisely the homogeneous polynomials of degree at most $d-3$, in accordance with the <adjunction formula> $K_C\cong\mathcal O_C(d-3)$.

= ii
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put
$$
F=X_0^4-X_1^4-X_2^4.
$$
By the <Jacobian criterion>, a <projective plane curve> is singular only at a common projective zero of $F$ and all its <partial derivatives>. Here
$$
F_{X_0}=4X_0^3,\qquad
F_{X_1}=-4X_1^3,\qquad
F_{X_2}=-4X_2^3.
$$
Their simultaneous vanishing forces $X_0=X_1=X_2=0$, which is not a point of <projective space>. Hence the Fermat quartic $C$ is <smooth projective curve>[nonsingular]. In particular it is a <smooth plane quartic> and has <geometric genus> $(4-1)(4-2)/2=3$.

= iii
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The intersection with a line $\ell$ is a <hyperplane section>:
$$
D=\ell\cap C,\qquad \mathcal O_C(D)\cong\mathcal O_C(1).
$$
The <canonical bundle of a smooth projective hypersurface> formula, obtained from the <adjunction formula>, gives for a smooth degree-four curve in $\mathbb P^2$
$$
K_C\cong
\left(K_{\mathbb P^2}\otimes\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^2}(4)\right)|_C
\cong\mathcal O_C(-3+4)
\cong\mathcal O_C(1).
$$
Thus $\mathcal O_C(D)\cong K_C$, so the <divisor class> of $D$ is the <canonical divisor> class. Therefore $D$ is a canonical divisor. Its degree is also $4=2g-2$, consistently with $g=3$.

= iv
{parent=24f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Choose a point $p\in C$ and project from $p$ to a line not containing $p$. Every line through $p$ meets the quartic in four points counted with <intersection multiplicity> by <Bézout theorem>; removing the fixed intersection at $p$ leaves a generic fibre of three points. This <projection from a point on a plane curve> therefore defines a nonconstant morphism
$$
C\longrightarrow\mathbb P^1
$$
of degree three, so the <gonality> is at most three.

It cannot be one, because a degree-one morphism between <smooth projective curves> is an <isomorphism of algebraic varieties>, whereas $C$ has genus three and the <projective line> has genus zero. It cannot be two either: that would make $C$ a <hyperelliptic curve>. The <canonical map of a hyperelliptic curve> is not a <closed immersion>[closed embedding], but for a <smooth plane quartic> the <canonical map of a smooth plane curve> is exactly its given embedding $C\hookrightarrow\mathbb P^2$, since $K_C\cong\mathcal O_C(1)$. This contradiction proves
$$
d=\operatorname{gon}(C)=3.
$$

= 25I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= a
{parent=25i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Suppose $S$ lies in the closed ball of radius $R$ centred at $c$. By <compactness>, the continuous function $q\mapsto|q-c|$ attains its maximum $r\leq R$ at some $p\in S$. The sphere of radius $r$ about $c$ is a <supporting sphere> tangent to $S$ at $p$.

Put $N=(p-c)/r$. For any unit vector $v\in T_pS$, choose a surface curve $\alpha$ with $\alpha(0)=p$ and $\alpha'(0)=v$. Since $|\alpha(t)-c|^2$ has a local maximum at zero,
$$
0\geq
\left.\frac{d^2}{dt^2}|\alpha(t)-c|^2\right|_{t=0}
=2+2r\langle\alpha''(0),N\rangle.
$$
The scalar $\langle\alpha''(0),N\rangle$ is the <normal curvature> in direction $v$, so every normal curvature is at most $-1/r$ with this choice of <unit normal>. Applying this to <principal direction>[principal directions] shows that both <principal curvature>[principal curvatures] satisfy $k_i\leq-1/r$. Therefore
$$
K(p)=k_1k_2\geq\frac1{r^2}\geq\frac1{R^2}>0.
$$
This proves both assertions, including the existence of an <elliptic point> on every compact regular surface.

= b
{parent=25i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose an <orthonormal basis> $(e_1,e_2)$ of $T_pS$ and write $e(\theta)=\cos\theta\,e_1+\sin\theta\,e_2$. On a punctured disc on which the <exponential map> is a <diffeomorphism>, the map
$$
X(r,\theta)=\exp_p\bigl(r e(\theta)\bigr),
\qquad 0<r<\varepsilon,
$$
defines <geodesic polar coordinates> centred at $p$. The curves $r\mapsto X(r,\theta)$ are unit-speed <geodesics>, so the first coefficient of the <first fundamental form> is
$$
E=\langle X_r,X_r\rangle=1.
$$
The <Gauss lemma> says that radial and angular coordinate vectors are orthogonal, hence
$$
F=\langle X_r,X_\theta\rangle=0.
$$

The angular variation $J=X_\theta$ is a <Jacobi field> along each radial geodesic, with initial data
$$
J(0)=0,
\qquad
D_rJ(0)=e'(\theta),
\qquad |e'(\theta)|=1.
$$
Since $G=\langle X_\theta,X_\theta\rangle=|J|^2$, smooth dependence of the <geodesic flow> gives
$$
\sqrt{G(r,\theta)}=|J(r)|=r+o(r).
$$
Consequently
$$
\lim_{r\to0}G(r,\theta)=0,
\qquad
\lim_{r\to0}(\sqrt G)_r(r,\theta)=1,
$$
as required.

= c
{parent=25i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $h(r,\theta)=\sqrt{G(r,\theta)}$. The <Jacobi equation in geodesic polar coordinates> and the initial conditions from part (b) are
$$
h_{rr}=-Kh,
\qquad h(0,\theta)=0,
\qquad h_r(0,\theta)=1.
$$
Inside a geodesic polar coordinate ball, $h>0$. If $K\leq0$, then $h_{rr}\geq0$, so $h_r\geq1$ and therefore $h(r,\theta)\geq r$. The <area element of a surface>[Riemannian area element] is $h\,dr\,d\theta$, whence
$$
\operatorname{Area}B(p,\varepsilon_0)
=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\varepsilon_0}h(r,\theta)\,dr\,d\theta
\geq\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\varepsilon_0}r\,dr\,d\theta
=\pi\varepsilon_0^2.
$$
Thus nonpositive <Gaussian curvature> makes such a ball at least as large as the <Euclidean disk> of the same radius.

= d
{parent=25i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Again set $h=\sqrt G$ and fix $\theta$. For $0<\alpha<C^{-1/2}$ define
$$
f(r)=h(r,\theta)-\alpha\sin(\sqrt C\,r).
$$
Since $h''=-Kh$ and $K\leq C$,
$$
f''+Cf=(C-K)h\geq0,
\qquad f(0)=0,
\qquad f'(0)=1-\alpha\sqrt C>0.
$$
For $0<r<\pi/\sqrt C$, differentiation gives
$$
\frac d{dr}
\left(f'\sin(\sqrt C r)-\sqrt C f\cos(\sqrt C r)\right)
=(f''+Cf)\sin(\sqrt C r)\geq0.
$$
The expression in parentheses vanishes at zero, and hence
$$
\left(\frac f{\sin(\sqrt C r)}\right)'\geq0.
$$
Its limit at zero is $(1-\alpha\sqrt C)/\sqrt C>0$, so $f>0$. Letting $\alpha\uparrow C^{-1/2}$ yields the <one-dimensional Rauch comparison inequality>
$$
h(r,\theta)\geq\frac{\sin(\sqrt C r)}{\sqrt C}
\qquad(0\leq r<\pi/\sqrt C).
$$
It follows that
$$
\operatorname{Area}B(p,\varepsilon)
\geq\frac{2\pi}{C}\bigl(1-\cos(\sqrt C\,\varepsilon)\bigr).
$$
Now
$$
\frac{2(1-\cos x)}{x^2}\longrightarrow1
$$
as $x\to0$. Given $0<\gamma<1$, choose $x_0\in(0,\pi)$ such that $2(1-\cos x)/x^2\geq\gamma$ for $0<x\leq x_0$, and set $\varepsilon_0=x_0/\sqrt C$. Then every geodesic polar coordinate ball of radius $\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_0$ satisfies
$$
\operatorname{Area}B(p,\varepsilon)\geq\gamma\pi\varepsilon^2.
$$

= 26K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <measure-preserving transformation> $T$ on a <probability space> $(X,\mathcal A,m)$ is <ergodic measure-preserving transformation>[ergodic] if every <invariant sigma-algebra>[invariant event] $A\in\mathcal A$, meaning
$$
T^{-1}A=A
$$
up to an $m$-null set, satisfies $m(A)\in\{0,1\}$. Thus the <invariant sigma-algebra> is trivial modulo null sets.

= b
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Kolmogorov zero-one law> says that for <independent random variables> $(Y_n)_{n\geq0}$, every event in the <tail sigma-algebra>
$$
\mathcal T=\bigcap_{N\geq0}\sigma(Y_N,Y_{N+1},\ldots)
$$
has <probability measure>[probability] zero or one.

In the <canonical model of an independent and identically distributed sequence>, the sample space is a sequence space $E^{\mathbb N}$ with the product law, $Y_n$ is the $n$th coordinate, and the left <Bernoulli shift> $S$ satisfies $Y_n\circ S=Y_{n+1}$. If $A$ is shift-invariant, then for every $N$,
$$
A=S^{-N}A\in\sigma(Y_N,Y_{N+1},\ldots),
$$
so $A\in\mathcal T$. The zero-one law therefore says that the canonical one-sided i.i.d. shift is <ergodic measure-preserving transformation>[ergodic].

= c
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a <Borel set> $A\subseteq[0,1]$, the two inverse branches of the <tent map> are $y\mapsto y/2$ and $y\mapsto1-y/2$. Apart from their immaterial common endpoint,
$$
T^{-1}A
=\frac A2\ \cup\ \left(1-\frac A2\right).
$$
Each branch scales <Lebesgue measure> by $1/2$, and their images lie in opposite half-intervals. Hence
$$
m(T^{-1}A)=\frac12m(A)+\frac12m(A)=m(A).
$$
Also $m([0,1])=1$, so $m$ is an invariant probability measure.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The event $\{X_n=1\}$ is $T^{-n}(0,1/2)$. Since $T^n$ has $2^n$ affine branches of slope magnitude $2^n$, this preimage is, up to endpoints, a disjoint union of $2^n$ intervals of length $2^{-(n+1)}$ and has measure $1/2$.

More generally, for every binary word $(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1})$, its <itinerary cylinder>
$$
C(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1})
=\{x:X_0(x)=a_0,\ldots,X_{N-1}(x)=a_{N-1}\}
$$
is, up to finitely many endpoints, one monotonicity interval of $T^N$ and has measure $2^{-N}$. Therefore
$$
m(C(a_0,\ldots,a_{N-1}))
=\prod_{j=0}^{N-1}m(X_j=a_j),
$$
so the $X_n$ are <independent and identically distributed random variables>, each with the <Bernoulli distribution> of parameter $1/2$.

The finite itinerary cylinders have diameters at most $2^{-N}$. Their endpoints form a dense set, so their unions generate the <Borel sigma-algebra> after <completion of a measure>[completion] by null sets. Thus
$$
\sigma(X_0,X_1,\ldots)=\mathcal A
$$
in the standard probability-space convention that σ-algebras are identified modulo null sets.

There is a small literal endpoint defect in the uncompleted formulation of the question: $0$, $1$, and $2/3$ all have the all-zero itinerary, so $\sigma(X_0,X_1,\ldots)$ cannot separate these Borel singletons. Consequently the displayed equality is false as an equality of raw Borel σ-algebras with the stated open interval, but it is true after completion and modulo null sets, which is the version used in the ergodicity argument.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Yes. Let $A$ be $T$-invariant. Since the itinerary variables generate $\mathcal A$ modulo null sets, $A$ can be regarded as an event in the canonical sequence model. For every $N$, invariance and $X_n\circ T=X_{n+1}$ imply
$$
A=T^{-N}A\in\sigma(X_N,X_{N+1},\ldots).
$$
Thus $A$ belongs to the <tail sigma-algebra> of the i.i.d. sequence $(X_n)$. By the <Kolmogorov zero-one law>, $m(A)$ is zero or one. Hence the <tent map> is an <ergodic measure-preserving transformation>.

= 27K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= Solution
{parent=27K}

A <renewal-reward process> consists of i.i.d. regenerative cycles $(\xi_n,R_n)$, where $\xi_n>0$ is the $n$th cycle length and $R_n$ is the reward accumulated during that cycle. If $0<\mathbb E\xi_1<\infty$ and $\mathbb E|R_1|<\infty$, the <renewal-reward theorem> states that the cumulative reward $Z(t)$ satisfies
$$
\frac{Z(t)}t\longrightarrow\frac{\mathbb E R_1}{\mathbb E\xi_1}
$$
almost surely, with the usual negligible-remainder condition for an unfinished cycle.

For the machine, let $X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$ be its working lifetime after a repair. The next repair occurs at the first inspection following failure, so one repair-to-repair cycle has length
$$
\xi=m\left\lceil\frac Xm\right\rceil,
$$
while its working-time reward is $R=X$. Since an <exponential distribution> of rate $\lambda$ has mean $1/\lambda$,
$$
\mathbb ER=\frac1\lambda.
$$
By the <tail-sum formula> for the positive integer-valued variable $\lceil X/m\rceil$,
$$
\mathbb E\left\lceil\frac Xm\right\rceil
=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\mathbb P(X>km)
=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}e^{-\lambda km}
=\frac1{1-e^{-\lambda m}}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\mathbb E\xi=\frac{m}{1-e^{-\lambda m}},
$$
and the <long-run availability under periodic inspection>[long-run proportion of time for which the machine is working] is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathbb ER}{\mathbb E\xi}
=\frac{1-e^{-\lambda m}}{\lambda m}}.
$$

= 28J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= i
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $K(t,\mathord\cdot)$ be the <Markov kernel> of the <Metropolis–Hastings algorithm>. Away from the possibility of remaining at $t$, its transition density is
$$
k(t,s)=q(s\mid t)\rho(t,s).
$$
Multiplication by the target density gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mu(t)k(t,s)
&=\mu(t)q(s\mid t)
\min\left\{\frac{\mu(s)q(t\mid s)}{\mu(t)q(s\mid t)},1\right\}\\
&=\min\{\mu(s)q(t\mid s),\mu(t)q(s\mid t)\}\\
&=\mu(s)k(s,t).
\end{aligned}
$$
This is <detailed balance> for every pair of distinct states.

Write
$$
r(t)=1-\int_\Theta k(t,s)\,ds
$$
for the rejection probability, so
$$
K(t,ds)=k(t,s)\,ds+r(t)\delta_t(ds).
$$
For every measurable $B\subseteq\Theta$, detailed balance and the <Tonelli theorem> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_\Theta K(t,B)\mu(t)\,dt
&=\int_B\int_\Theta \mu(t)k(t,s)\,dt\,ds
 +\int_B\mu(t)r(t)\,dt\\
&=\int_B\mu(s)\left[\int_\Theta k(s,t)\,dt+r(s)\right]ds\\
&=\int_B\mu(s)\,ds.
\end{aligned}
$$
Hence $\mu$ is an <invariant distribution> of the chain.

= ii
{parent=28j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $L(t)=f(x,t)$ be the <likelihood function>. Since the <prior distribution> is $N(0,I_p)$, the <posterior density> has the form
$$
\mu(t)=\frac1ZL(t)\phi(t),
\qquad
\phi(t)=(2\pi)^{-p/2}e^{-\lVert t\rVert^2/2},
$$
where $Z$ is the <Bayesian model evidence>[normalizing constant].

Choose $0<\delta\leq1/2$ and use the <Gaussian autoregressive proposal reversible with respect to a standard normal distribution>
$$
s=\sqrt{1-2\delta}\,t+\sqrt{2\delta}\,Z_0,
\qquad Z_0\sim N(0,I_p).
$$
Thus
$$
q(\mathord\cdot\mid t)
=N\!\left(\sqrt{1-2\delta}\,t,2\delta I_p\right),
$$
whose <covariance matrix> is the required $2\delta I_p$. Reversibility with respect to the <standard normal distribution> says
$$
\phi(t)q(s\mid t)=\phi(s)q(t\mid s),
$$
so
$$
\frac{q(t\mid s)}{q(s\mid t)}=\frac{\phi(t)}{\phi(s)}.
$$
The <Metropolis–Hastings acceptance probability> therefore reduces to
$$
\rho(t,s)
=\min\left\{
\frac{L(s)\phi(s)}{L(t)\phi(t)}
\frac{\phi(t)}{\phi(s)},1
\right\}
=\boxed{\min\left\{\frac{f(x,s)}{f(x,t)},1\right\}}.
$$
This is the <preconditioned Crank–Nicolson algorithm> with proposal scale $\beta=\sqrt{2\delta}$.

= 29K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A real-valued process $(B_t)_{t\geq0}$ is a standard <Brownian motion> if:

* $B_0=0$ almost surely;
* it has <independent increments>;
* $B_t-B_s$ has the <normal distribution> $N(0,t-s)$ whenever $0\leq s<t$; and
* its sample paths are almost surely <continuous function>[continuous].

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $M_t=\sup_{0\leq s\leq t}B_s$ and let $a>0$. The <Brownian reflection principle> says that reflecting a Brownian path about the level $a$ after its first hitting time of $a$ preserves its law. In particular, for $b\leq a$,
$$
\mathbb P(M_t\geq a,\ B_t\leq b)
=\mathbb P(B_t\geq2a-b).
$$
Taking $b=a$ gives
$$
\mathbb P(M_t\geq a)=2\mathbb P(B_t\geq a).
$$

= b
{parent=29k}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $\Phi$ for the <standard normal cumulative distribution function>. Since $B_t\leq M_t$, for $y\geq0$ and $x>y$,
$$
\mathbb P(B_t\leq x,M_t\leq y)
=\mathbb P(M_t\leq y)
=2\Phi\!\left(\frac y{\sqrt t}\right)-1.
$$
For $x\leq y$, the <Brownian reflection principle> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb P(B_t\leq x,M_t\leq y)
&=\mathbb P(B_t\leq x)
-\mathbb P(B_t\leq x,M_t>y)\\
&=\Phi\!\left(\frac x{\sqrt t}\right)
-\mathbb P(B_t>2y-x)\\
&=\Phi\!\left(\frac x{\sqrt t}\right)
+\Phi\!\left(\frac{2y-x}{\sqrt t}\right)-1.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <joint distribution function> is therefore
$$
\boxed{
F_{B_t,M_t}(x,y)=
\begin{cases}
0,&y<0,\\
\Phi(x/\sqrt t)+\Phi((2y-x)/\sqrt t)-1,&y\geq0,\ x\leq y,\\
2\Phi(y/\sqrt t)-1,&y\geq0,\ x>y.
\end{cases}}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the interior of the support $b<m$, differentiating the <joint distribution of Brownian motion and its running maximum> gives the <joint probability density>
$$
f_{B_t,M_t}(b,m)
=\frac{2(2m-b)}{\sqrt{2\pi}\,t^{3/2}}
\exp\!\left(-\frac{(2m-b)^2}{2t}\right).
$$
Make the <change of variables formula for a probability density> $y=m$ and $z=m-b$. Its inverse is $m=y$, $b=y-z$, and its <Jacobian determinant> has absolute value one. Since $2m-b=y+z$, for $y,z\geq0$ this yields
$$
\boxed{
f_{M_t,Z_t}(y,z)
=\frac{2(y+z)}{\sqrt{2\pi t}\,t}
e^{-(y+z)^2/(2t)}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The two processes with the same distribution are
$$
\boxed{(|B_t|)_{t\geq0}\ \overset d=\ (Z_t)_{t\geq0}}.
$$
Indeed, $(M_t)_{t\geq0}$ has almost surely <monotone function>[nondecreasing] paths, whereas $|B_t|$ and $M_t-B_t$ do not, so $M$ cannot be one member of the equal pair. The stated conclusion then follows from the premise that two of the three processes have the same law. More directly, it is the first coordinate of the <Lévy identity for Brownian motion>; both $|B|$ and $M-B$ are <reflected Brownian motion>.

= 30D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= a
{parent=30d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <phase function> $\cos t$ attains its global maximum $1$ on $[0,3\pi]$ at the stationary endpoint $t=0$ and at the interior point $t=2\pi$. At either point,
$$
\cos(t_0+u)=1-\frac{u^2}{2}+O(u^4).
$$
By <Laplace's method at a nondegenerate stationary endpoint>, the endpoint contributes
$$
e^x\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2x}}.
$$
By <Laplace's method>, the interior maximum contributes
$$
e^{x+2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{x}}.
$$
The remaining parts of the interval are exponentially smaller. Summing the two contributions according to <Laplace's method with multiple global maxima> gives
$$
\boxed{
I(x)\sim e^x\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2x}}
\left(1+2e^{2\pi}\right)}.
$$

= b
{parent=30d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set
$$
u=\log(1+t),
\qquad t=e^u-1,
\qquad dt=e^u\,du.
$$
Then
$$
J(x)=\int_0^{\log(1+\pi)}g(u)e^{-xu}\,du,
\qquad
g(u)=e^u\bigl[1-\cos(e^u-1)\bigr].
$$
The <Taylor series> at the endpoint is
$$
e^u-1=u+\frac{u^2}{2}+\frac{u^3}{6}+O(u^4),
$$
and hence
$$
g(u)=\frac{u^2}{2}+u^3+O(u^4).
$$
The <Watson lemma>, together with
$$
\int_0^\infty u^ne^{-xu}\,du=\frac{n!}{x^{n+1}},
$$
therefore yields
$$
\boxed{J(x)=\frac1{x^3}+\frac6{x^4}+O(x^{-5})}.
$$

= 31E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= a
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\phi_t(x_0)$ denote the <flow map> generated by $\dot x=f(x)$.

* The fixed point $0$ is <asymptotic stability>[asymptotically stable] if it is <Lyapunov stability>[Lyapunov stable] and there is a neighbourhood $U$ of $0$ such that $\phi_t(x_0)\to0$ as $t\to\infty$ for every $x_0\in U$.
* Its <domain of stability>, or <basin of attraction>, is $\{x_0:\phi_t(x_0)\to0\text{ as }t\to\infty\}$, with forward existence understood.
* A <Lyapunov function> on a neighbourhood $E$ of $0$ is a continuous function $V$, differentiable away from the origin, such that $V(0)=0$, $V(x)>0$ for $x\ne0$, and its <orbital derivative>
$$
\dot V(x)=\nabla V(x)\mathbin{\cdot}f(x)
$$
is nonpositive on $E$.

The <First Lyapunov theorem> says that the existence of such a function makes the origin Lyapunov stable. To prove it, choose a sufficiently small closed ball $\overline B_\varepsilon\subset E$. By <compactness> and positive definiteness,
$$
\alpha=\min_{\lVert x\rVert=\varepsilon}V(x)>0.
$$
By <continuity>, some $\delta>0$ satisfies $V(x)<\alpha$ whenever $\lVert x\rVert<\delta$. Along a trajectory beginning in $B_\delta$, $V$ cannot increase. Such a trajectory therefore cannot reach the sphere $\lVert x\rVert=\varepsilon$, where $V\geq\alpha$. Hence it remains in $B_\varepsilon$ for every $t\geq0$, which is Lyapunov stability.

The <LaSalle invariance principle> says that if a trajectory remains in a compact <positively invariant set> on which $\dot V\leq0$, then it approaches the largest invariant subset of $\{x:\dot V(x)=0\}$.

= b
{parent=31e}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the two vertical sides of the square, the $x$ component of the <vector field> satisfies
$$
\dot x\big|_{x=1}=-1+\sin(2y)\leq0,
\qquad
\dot x\big|_{x=-1}=1+\sin(2y)\geq0.
$$
On the horizontal sides,
$$
\dot y\big|_{y=1}=-x-1\leq0,
\qquad
\dot y\big|_{y=-1}=1-x\geq0.
$$
Thus the vector field never points outward through the boundary, so the closed square is a <positively invariant set> and trajectories cannot leave $S$.

At a fixed point, $\dot y=0$ gives $x=-y^3$. Substitution into $\dot x=0$ gives
$$
2y^3-y^9+\sin(2y)=0.
$$
For $0<y<1$, both $y^3(2-y^6)$ and $\sin(2y)$ are positive; for $-1<y<0$, both are negative. Hence $y=0$, and then $x=0$, is the unique <equilibrium point> in $S$.

This alone does not prove that $S$ lies in the <domain of stability>. A compact trapping region containing a unique equilibrium can also contain a <periodic orbit> or another nontrivial <omega-limit set>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For
$$
V(x,y)=\frac{x^2}{2}+g(y),
$$
the <orbital derivative> is
$$
\dot V=x(-2x+x^3+\sin2y)+g'(y)(-x-y^3).
$$
Choosing $g'(y)=\sin2y$ cancels the mixed term. Taking $g(0)=0$ gives
$$
g(y)=\int_0^y\sin(2s)\,ds
=\frac{1-\cos2y}{2}=\sin^2y,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{V(x,y)=\frac{x^2}{2}+\sin^2y}.
$$
This function has <positive definiteness of a Lyapunov function>[positive definiteness] near the origin, and
$$
\dot V
=-2x^2+x^4-y^3\sin2y
=-x^2(2-x^2)-y^3\sin2y.
$$
For $|x|<1$ and $|y|<1$, both terms are nonpositive and equality holds only at $(0,0)$. Thus $V$ is a <strict Lyapunov function> near the origin, and the <Second Lyapunov theorem> proves that the origin is asymptotically stable.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The factor $x^2(2-x^2)$ is positive for $0<|x|<\sqrt2$. The product $y^3\sin2y$ is positive for $0<|y|<\pi/2$. Therefore $\dot V<0$ away from the origin throughout
$$
\boxed{|x|<x_0=\sqrt2,
\qquad |y|<y_0=\frac\pi2}.
$$
Neither side length can be enlarged while retaining a strict Lyapunov function on the whole rectangle: at $(x,0)$ with $|x|>\sqrt2$, the first term makes $\dot V>0$, while at $(0,y)$ immediately beyond $|y|=\pi/2$, the second term makes $\dot V>0$.

Strict decrease on this rectangle does not by itself put the whole rectangle in the <domain of stability>, because the rectangle has not been shown to be <positively invariant set>[positively invariant]; a trajectory may leave it before convergence.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

To obtain a <basin estimate from a Lyapunov sublevel set>, take the largest sublevel component around the origin that remains inside the strict-decrease rectangle. On its boundary $|x|=\sqrt2$ or $|y|=\pi/2$, the minimum value of $V$ is $1$. Hence the maximal open estimate is
$$
\boxed{
\mathcal D_V=
\left\{(x,y): |y|<\frac\pi2,
\ \frac{x^2}{2}+\sin^2y<1\right\}}.
$$
Every smaller closed sublevel set $V\leq c<1$ is compact and positively invariant, and $\dot V<0$ there except at the origin. The <LaSalle invariance principle> therefore implies convergence to the origin. Taking the union over $c<1$ shows that $\mathcal D_V$ lies in the domain of stability. No larger sublevel component can be certified from this $V$ alone, because every level $c>1$ contains points $(0,y)$ just beyond $|y|=\pi/2$ where $\dot V>0$.

= 32C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= a
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\xi=V_1$, $\eta=\phi$, and $u_j=d^ju/dx^j$. On the <jet space of a scalar ordinary differential equation>, define
$$
\eta^{(0)}=\eta,
\qquad
\eta^{(j)}=D_x\eta^{(j-1)}-u_jD_x\xi,
$$
where the <total derivative operator> is
$$
D_x=\partial_x+u_1\partial_u+u_2\partial_{u_1}+\cdots.
$$
The order-$N$ <prolongation of a vector field> is
$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{pr}^{(N)}V
=\xi\partial_x+\eta\partial_u
+\sum_{j=1}^N\eta^{(j)}\partial_{u_j}}.
$$

In the coordinates $(x,u)$, the given action of the <special orthogonal group> is
$$
\widetilde x=x\cos s+u\sin s,
\qquad
\widetilde u=u\cos s-x\sin s.
$$
Differentiating at $s=0$ gives the <infinitesimal generator of a Lie point symmetry>
$$
V=u\partial_x-x\partial_u,
$$
so $\xi=u$ and $\eta=-x$. The recursive formula gives
$$
\eta^{(1)}=D_x(-x)-u_xD_xu=-1-u_x^2
$$
and
$$
\eta^{(2)}=D_x(-1-u_x^2)-u_{xx}D_xu
=-3u_xu_{xx}.
$$
Therefore the <second prolongation of the rotation generator for plane graphs> is
$$
\boxed{
\operatorname{pr}^{(2)}V
=u\partial_x-x\partial_u
-(1+u_x^2)\partial_{u_x}
-3u_xu_{xx}\partial_{u_{xx}}}.
$$

For $\Delta_0=u_{xx}$,
$$
\operatorname{pr}^{(2)}V(\Delta_0)=-3u_xu_{xx},
$$
which vanishes when $\Delta_0=0$. For
$$
\Delta_1=u_{xx}-(1+u_x^2)^{3/2},
$$
we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{pr}^{(2)}V(\Delta_1)
&=-3u_xu_{xx}
-3u_x(1+u_x^2)^{1/2}[-(1+u_x^2)]\\
&=-3u_x\Delta_1,
\end{aligned}
$$
which likewise vanishes on the equation. This is the infinitesimal invariance criterion for both equations.

Geometrically, $u_{xx}=0$ describes straight lines. The <signed curvature of a plane graph> is
$$
\kappa=\frac{u_{xx}}{(1+u_x^2)^{3/2}},
$$
so the second equation says $\kappa=1$ and describes consistently oriented arcs of unit circles. <rotation matrix>[Rotation] preserves straight lines, circles, and signed curvature, explaining both invariances.

= b
{parent=32c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The transformations
$$
g^s:(X,T,u)\longmapsto(e^sX,e^{-s}T,u)
$$
form a <one-parameter subgroup>[one-parameter group] with infinitesimal generator
$$
V=X\partial_X-T\partial_T.
$$
Under this scaling, $\partial_X$ has weight $-1$ and $\partial_T$ has weight $1$, so $u_{XT}$ has weight zero, as does $\sin u$. Thus $u_{XT}=\sin u$ is invariant and $g^s$ is a <scaling symmetry of the sine-Gordon equation>.

The product
$$
z=XT
$$
is invariant under the group. A <group-invariant solution> therefore has the form $u(X,T)=F(z)$. Since
$$
u_X=TF'(z),
\qquad
u_{XT}=F'(z)+zF''(z),
$$
the <Sine-Gordon equation> reduces to
$$
zF''+F'=\sin F.
$$

Now put $w=e^{iF}$. Then
$$
F'=-i\frac{w'}w,
\qquad
F''=-i\left(\frac{w''}w-\frac{(w')^2}{w^2}\right),
\qquad
\sin F=\frac{w-w^{-1}}{2i}.
$$
Substitution and multiplication by $iw$ give
$$
zw''-z\frac{(w')^2}{w}+w'
=\frac{w^2-1}{2}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{
w''=\frac{(w')^2}{w}-\frac{w'}z+\frac{w^2-1}{2z}}.
$$
This is the <Painlevé III equation> with parameters $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta)=(1/2,-1/2,0,0)$.

= 33A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33A}

A <boson> is an identical particle whose total <multiparticle quantum state> is symmetric under exchange of any two particles, whereas a <fermion> has a total state that is antisymmetric under every exchange. The <spin-statistics theorem> associates integer spin with bosons and half-integer spin with fermions.

For three distinguishable spin-one particles, the spin <Hilbert space> has dimension
$$
3^3=27.
$$
Let
$$
\mathbf S=\mathbf S_1+\mathbf S_2+\mathbf S_3
$$
be the <total angular momentum operator>. Since each particle has
$$
\mathbf S_i^2=1(1+1)\hbar^2=2\hbar^2,
$$
we have
$$
\mathbf S^2=6\hbar^2
+2(\mathbf S_1\mathbin\cdot\mathbf S_2
+\mathbf S_2\mathbin\cdot\mathbf S_3
+\mathbf S_3\mathbin\cdot\mathbf S_1).
$$
The <Hamiltonian operator> is consequently
$$
H=\frac\lambda{\hbar^2}(\mathbf S^2-6\hbar^2).
$$
On the <total-spin sector> with quantum number $J$, its <energy eigenvalue> is
$$
E_J=\lambda[J(J+1)-6].
$$

The <Clebsch-Gordan decomposition> may be performed by first coupling particles $1$ and $2$. Their intermediate spin is $j_{12}=0,1,2$, and coupling the third spin gives
$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
j_{12}&J\\ \hline
0&1\\
1&0,1,2\\
2&1,2,3.
\end{array}
$$
Thus the <three spin-one angular-momentum decomposition> contains total spin $J=0,1,2,3$ with multiplicities $1,3,2,1$. Multiplying each multiplicity by the multiplet dimension $2J+1$ gives
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
J&E_J&\text{degeneracy}\\ \hline
0&-6\lambda&1\\
1&-4\lambda&9\\
2&0&10\\
3&6\lambda&7.
\end{array}
$$
The degeneracies sum to $27$, as required.

When the particles are indistinguishable, their integer spin makes them bosons. Their common spatial wavefunction is symmetric, so their spin wavefunction must also lie in the <symmetric three-spin-one subspace>. Its decomposition is
$$
\operatorname{Sym}^3(1)=3\oplus1.
$$
Hence only the $J=3$ and $J=1$ levels remain, now with one copy of each multiplet:
$$
\boxed{
E=-4\lambda\text{ has degeneracy }3,
\qquad
E=6\lambda\text{ has degeneracy }7.}
$$

= 34C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=34c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a real short-range <spherically symmetric potential>, the stationary <wavefunction> must solve the <Schrodinger equation>, be regular at the origin whenever the potential is finite there, and satisfy the scattering boundary condition
$$
\psi(\mathbf r)\sim e^{ikz}+f(\theta)\frac{e^{ikr}}r
\qquad(r\to\infty).
$$
Here $e^{ikz}$ is the incident <plane wave>, the second term is an outgoing spherical wave, and its coefficient $f(\theta)$ is the <scattering amplitude>.

Comparing the stated asymptotic expression with the <partial-wave expansion of a scattering amplitude> gives
$$
f(\theta)=\frac1k\sum_{l=0}^{\infty}(2l+1)f_lP_l(\cos\theta),
$$
where $P_l$ is a <Legendre polynomial>. The outgoing-to-incoming coefficient in channel $l$ is
$$
S_l=1+2if_l.
$$
<Partial-wave unitarity> requires
$$
|S_l|=1,
$$
or equivalently
$$
\operatorname{Im}f_l=|f_l|^2.
$$
The real <scattering phase shift> $\delta_l$ is defined modulo $\pi$ by
$$
S_l=e^{2i\delta_l},
\qquad
f_l=\frac{e^{2i\delta_l}-1}{2i}
=e^{i\delta_l}\sin\delta_l.
$$

= b
{parent=34c}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For <S-wave scattering>, write the radial wavefunction as $R_0(r)=u(r)/r$. In the attractive shell $a<r\leq2a$, the hard-core boundary condition $u(a)=0$ gives
$$
u_{\mathrm{in}}(r)=A\sin[\kappa(r-a)],
\qquad
\kappa^2=k^2+\frac{2mV_0}{\hbar^2}.
$$
Outside the potential, choose
$$
u_{\mathrm{out}}(r)=B\sin(kr+\delta_0).
$$
Continuity of the logarithmic derivative at $r=2a$ yields
$$
\kappa\cot(\kappa a)
=k\cot(2ka+\delta_0).
$$
Put $C=\kappa\cot(\kappa a)$. The <tangent addition formula> gives
$$
\tan(2ka+\delta_0)=\frac{k}{C},
$$
and solving for $\tan\delta_0$ gives
$$
\tan\delta_0
=\frac{k-C\tan(2ka)}{C+k\tan(2ka)}
=\boxed{
\frac{k\cos(2ka)-\kappa\cot(\kappa a)\sin(2ka)}
{k\sin(2ka)+\kappa\cot(\kappa a)\cos(2ka)}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let
$$
\kappa_0=\lim_{k\to0}\kappa
=\frac{\sqrt{2mV_0}}\hbar.
$$
Using $\sin(2ka)=2ka+O(k^3)$ and $\cos(2ka)=1+O(k^2)$ in the phase-shift formula gives
$$
\frac{\tan\delta_0}{k}
\longrightarrow
\frac1{\kappa_0\cot(\kappa_0a)}-2a
=\frac{\tan(\kappa_0a)}{\kappa_0}-2a.
$$
By the definition of the <scattering length from a partial-wave S-matrix>,
$$
\boxed{
a_s=-\lim_{k\to0}\frac{\tan\delta_0}{k}
=2a-\frac{\tan(\kappa_0a)}{\kappa_0}}.
$$
Thus $a_s$ diverges exactly when
$$
\boxed{\kappa_0a=\left(n+\frac12\right)\pi,
\qquad n=0,1,2,\ldots}.
$$
At each such value, an S-wave <bound state> lies at the continuum threshold: the exterior zero-energy radial solution becomes constant, its logarithmic derivative vanishes, and the system has a <zero-energy scattering resonance>. As the well deepens through the critical value, a new bound state appears and the scattering length passes through infinity and changes sign.

= 35A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= i
{parent=35a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In a box of volume $V$, the <electromagnetic mode density in a box>, including the two transverse <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>[polarizations], is
$$
dN=2\frac{V}{(2\pi)^3}d^3k
=\frac{V}{\pi^2}k^2\,dk.
$$
Each <photon> has energy $\hbar\omega=\hbar ck$, and the given <Planck photon distribution>[mean occupation number] is $(e^{\hbar ck/T}-1)^{-1}$. Omitting the temperature-independent <zero-point energy>, the total energy is therefore
$$
E=\frac{V\hbar c}{\pi^2}
\int_0^\infty\frac{k^3}{e^{\hbar ck/T}-1}\,dk.
$$
With the <change of variables> $x=\hbar ck/T$,
$$
E=\frac{VT^4}{\pi^2\hbar^3c^3}
\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}\,dx.
$$

= ii
{parent=35a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expanding $(e^x-1)^{-1}=\sum_{n\geq1}e^{-nx}$ and using the <Gamma integral> gives
$$
\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}\,dx
=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\int_0^\infty x^3e^{-nx}\,dx
=6\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^4}
=\frac{\pi^4}{15}.
$$
Thus the energy of <blackbody radiation> is
$$
\boxed{E(T,V)=\frac{\pi^2}{15\hbar^3c^3}VT^4}.
$$

= iii
{parent=35a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <first law of thermodynamics> is
$$
dE=T\,dS-P\,dV,
$$
so, regarding $S$ as a function of $(T,V)$,
$$
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T}\right)_V
=\frac1T\left(\frac{\partial E}{\partial T}\right)_V,
\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T
=\frac1T\left[
\left(\frac{\partial E}{\partial V}\right)_T+P
\right].
$$
Equality of mixed <partial derivative>[partial derivatives] gives the <thermodynamic derivation of blackbody radiation pressure>
$$
\boxed{
\left(\frac{\partial E}{\partial V}\right)_T
=T\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_V-P}.
$$
Writing $a=\pi^2/(15\hbar^3c^3)$, we have $E=aVT^4$, and hence
$$
T\frac{dP}{dT}-P=aT^4.
$$
Equivalently, $d(P/T)/dT=aT^2$, so
$$
P=\frac a3T^4+CT.
$$
The condition $P=o(T)$ as $T\to0$ forces $C=0$. Therefore the <radiation pressure> is
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{\pi^2T^4}{45\hbar^3c^3}
=\frac{E}{3V}}.
$$

= 36D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Electrodynamics}

= i
{parent=36d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $\boldsymbol\sigma$ be the stated <Maxwell stress tensor>. Taking its divergence and using
$$
(\mathbf A\mathbin\cdot\nabla)\mathbf A
-\frac12\nabla|\mathbf A|^2
=-\mathbf A\times(\nabla\times\mathbf A)
$$
gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\partial_j\sigma_{ij}
={}&\epsilon_0[\mathbf E\times(\nabla\times\mathbf E)]_i
-\epsilon_0E_i\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf E\\
&+\frac1{\mu_0}[\mathbf B\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B)]_i
-\frac1{\mu_0}B_i\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <Maxwell equations> in vacuum with sources are
$$
\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf E=\frac\rho{\epsilon_0},
\qquad
\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0,
\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\partial_t\mathbf B,
\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J+\mu_0\epsilon_0\partial_t\mathbf E.
$$
Substitution, together with antisymmetry of the <cross product>, yields
$$
\begin{aligned}
\partial_j\sigma_{ij}
&=-\rho E_i-(\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)_i
-\epsilon_0[\mathbf E\times\partial_t\mathbf B
+\partial_t\mathbf E\times\mathbf B]_i\\
&=-[\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B]_i
-\partial_t[\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B]_i.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore the required vector is the <electromagnetic momentum density>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B
=\frac{\mathbf S}{c^2}},
$$
and
$$
\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^3\frac{\partial\sigma_{ij}}{\partial x_j}
+\frac{\partial g_i}{\partial t}
=-(\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)_i}.
$$
This is <local conservation of electromagnetic momentum>. The tensor component $\sigma_{ij}$ is the flux of $i$-momentum in the $j$ direction in the convention of the question, $\mathbf g$ is field momentum per unit volume, and $\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B$ is the <Lorentz force density> transferred to matter.

= ii
{parent=36d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the <radiation zone>, both fields are transverse to $\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and obey
$$
\mathbf E_0=-c\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf B_0,
\qquad
|\mathbf E_0|=c|\mathbf B_0|.
$$
The outward <Poynting vector> is
$$
\mathbf S
=\frac1{\mu_0}\mathbf E\times\mathbf B
=\frac c{\mu_0}|\mathbf B_0|^2
\sin^2(\omega t-\mathbf k\mathbin\cdot\mathbf x)
\widehat{\mathbf x},
$$
so its <Eulerian time average>[time average] is
$$
\langle S_r\rangle=\frac c{2\mu_0}|\mathbf B_0|^2.
$$
An absorbing shell receives the incident momentum rather than reversing it. Hence <radiation pressure on a perfectly absorbing surface> gives
$$
p(\widehat{\mathbf x})
=\frac{\langle S_r\rangle}{c}
=\frac{|\mathbf B_0|^2}{2\mu_0}.
$$
At $r=R$,
$$
|\mathbf B_0|^2
=\frac{\mu_0^2\omega^4}{16\pi^2R^2c^2}
|\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf p_0|^2,
$$
and therefore
$$
\boxed{
p(\widehat{\mathbf x})
=\frac{\mu_0\omega^4}{32\pi^2R^2c^2}
|\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\mathbf p_0|^2}.
$$
If $\vartheta$ is the angle between $\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and $\mathbf p_0$, this is
$$
p(\vartheta)=
\frac{\mu_0\omega^4|\mathbf p_0|^2}
{32\pi^2R^2c^2}\sin^2\vartheta.
$$
Its spherical average is $\mu_0\omega^4|\mathbf p_0|^2/(48\pi^2R^2c^2)$; the vector force integrates to zero by the inversion symmetry of the <electric dipole radiation> pattern.

= 37D
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= a
{parent=37d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In coordinates $x^\mu$, write the type-$(1,1)$ tensor as
$$
T=T^\mu{}_{\nu}\,
\partial_\mu\otimes dx^\nu.
$$
For a covector $\eta=\eta_\mu dx^\mu$ and vector $V=V^\nu\partial_\nu$,
$$
T(\eta,V)=T^\mu{}_{\nu}\eta_\mu V^\nu.
$$
Since this must equal $\eta(V)=\eta_\nu V^\nu$ for every $\eta$ and $V$, the components are
$$
\boxed{T^\mu{}_{\nu}=\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}}.
$$
Thus $T$ is the <identity tensor>.

The <tensor component transformation law> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
T^{\mu'}{}_{\nu'}
&=\frac{\partial x^{\mu'}}{\partial x^\alpha}
\frac{\partial x^\beta}{\partial x^{\nu'}}
T^\alpha{}_{\beta}\\
&=\frac{\partial x^{\mu'}}{\partial x^\alpha}
\frac{\partial x^\alpha}{\partial x^{\nu'}}\\
&=\delta^{\mu'}{}_{\nu'}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The components are therefore the same <Kronecker delta> in every coordinate system.

= b
{parent=37d}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put
$$
\rho=\sqrt{r^2+a^2},
\qquad
\varpi=\rho\sin\theta,
\qquad
z=r\cos\theta.
$$
Since $x^2+y^2=\varpi^2$, the Euclidean spatial line element is
$$
dx^2+dy^2+dz^2=d\varpi^2+\varpi^2d\phi^2+dz^2.
$$
Now
$$
d\varpi=\frac r\rho\sin\theta\,dr
+\rho\cos\theta\,d\theta,
\qquad
dz=\cos\theta\,dr-r\sin\theta\,d\theta.
$$
The cross terms cancel. With
$$
\Sigma=r^2+a^2\cos^2\theta,
\qquad
\Delta=r^2+a^2,
$$
we obtain the <oblate spheroidal coordinates> line element
$$
dx^2+dy^2+dz^2
=\frac\Sigma\Delta dr^2+\Sigma d\theta^2
+\Delta\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2.
$$
Adding the time coordinate gives
$$
\boxed{
ds^2=-dt^2
+\frac{r^2+a^2\cos^2\theta}{r^2+a^2}dr^2
+(r^2+a^2\cos^2\theta)d\theta^2
+(r^2+a^2)\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $u=t-r$ and $R=r$, so
$$
dt=du+dR,
\qquad dr=dR.
$$
Writing now $\Sigma=R^2+a^2\cos^2\theta$ and $\Delta=R^2+a^2$, the <Minkowski metric> becomes
$$
ds^2=-du^2-2\,du\,dR
-\frac{a^2\sin^2\theta}{\Delta}dR^2
+\Sigma d\theta^2
+\Delta\sin^2\theta\,d\phi^2.
$$
The squared norm of the coordinate vector is its $RR$ metric component:
$$
g(\partial_R,\partial_R)=g_{RR}
=-\frac{a^2\sin^2\theta}{R^2+a^2}.
$$
For $a>0$ this is nonzero away from the symmetry axis, so $\partial_R$ is not a <null vector>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $\varphi=\phi-F(R)$, so
$$
d\phi=d\varphi+F'(R)dR.
$$
The new coefficient of $dR^2$ is
$$
-\frac{a^2\sin^2\theta}{\Delta}
+\Delta\sin^2\theta\,[F'(R)]^2.
$$
It vanishes if
$$
F'(R)=\frac a{R^2+a^2}.
$$
One choice that tends to zero as $R\to\infty$ and also has a regular $a\to0$ limit is
$$
\boxed{F(R)=-\arctan\frac aR}.
$$
Substitution gives the <outgoing null oblate spheroidal coordinates> form
$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
ds^2={}&-du^2-2\,du\,dR
+2a\sin^2\theta\,dR\,d\varphi\\
&+(R^2+a^2\cos^2\theta)d\theta^2
+(R^2+a^2)\sin^2\theta\,d\varphi^2.
\end{aligned}}
$$
There is no $dR^2$ term, so $g_{RR}=0$ and $\partial_R$ is null for every $a\geq0$.

= 38B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= a
{parent=38b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose $x$ tangential to the solid boundary and locally along the outer inviscid streamlines, and let $y$ be the inward normal coordinate. Write $u$ and $v$ for the corresponding tangential and normal <velocity field> components, $U(x)$ for the outer tangential velocity, and $\nu$ for the <kinematic viscosity>.

For a layer of streamwise scale $L$ and thickness $\delta\ll L$, <incompressible flow> gives
$$
u_x+v_y=0,
$$
so $u=O(U)$ and $v=O(U\delta/L)$. The normal <Navier-Stokes equation> then gives $p_y=0$ to leading order, while the outer <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>[Euler equations] give
$$
-\frac1\rho p_x=U\frac{dU}{dx}.
$$
In the tangential Navier-Stokes equation, streamwise viscous diffusion is smaller than normal diffusion by $O((\delta/L)^2)$. Retaining the leading inertial and normal-diffusion terms produces the <Prandtl boundary-layer equation>
$$
\boxed{u u_x+v u_y=U\frac{dU}{dx}+\nu u_{yy}}.
$$
It is supplemented by $u=v=0$ at a stationary wall and $u\to U(x)$ on matching to the outer flow.

= b
{parent=38b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Conservation of volume flux through a circular arc of radius $r$ gives
$$
\alpha r(-u_r)=\alpha Q,
$$
so the outer flow is the <sink flow in a sector>
$$
\boxed{u_r=-\frac Qr,
\qquad u_\theta=0,
\qquad \psi=-Q\theta}.
$$
This is a <potential flow> and already satisfies the <no-penetration boundary condition> on the radial walls. It has nonzero tangential speed $Q/r$ there, however, and therefore violates the <no-slip boundary condition>. Thin vorticity-bearing <boundary layer>[boundary layers] are needed to reduce that speed to zero at each wall.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Along $y=0$, put $x=r$ and $U(x)=-Q/x$. If $\delta(x)$ is the layer thickness, then
$$
u u_x=O\!\left(\frac{Q^2}{x^3}\right),
\qquad
\nu u_{yy}=O\!\left(\frac{\nu Q}{x\delta^2}\right).
$$
The <boundary-layer thickness from an advection-diffusion balance> therefore gives
$$
\frac{Q^2}{x^3}\sim\frac{\nu Q}{x\delta^2},
$$
and hence
$$
\boxed{\delta=x\sqrt{\frac\nu Q}}
$$
up to a constant factor absorbed into the similarity coordinate.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let
$$
\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac\nu Q},
\qquad
\eta=\frac{y}{\epsilon x},
\qquad
\psi=\sqrt{\nu Q}\,f(\eta)=Q\epsilon f(\eta).
$$
Using the <Cartesian streamfunction> convention $u=\psi_y$, $v=-\psi_x$ gives
$$
u=\frac Qx f'(\eta),
\qquad
v=\frac{Q\epsilon}{x}\eta f'(\eta).
$$
At fixed $y$,
$$
u_x=-\frac Q{x^2}[f'+\eta f''],
\qquad
u_y=\frac Q{\epsilon x^2}f'',
\qquad
u_{yy}=\frac Q{\epsilon^2x^3}f'''.
$$
The terms proportional to $\eta f'f''$ cancel, leaving
$$
u u_x+v u_y=-\frac{Q^2}{x^3}(f')^2.
$$
Since $U=-Q/x$, $UU'=-Q^2/x^3$, while $\nu u_{yy}=Q^2f'''/x^3$. The boundary-layer equation therefore becomes
$$
\boxed{f'''=1-(f')^2}.
$$
Choosing the wall value of the streamfunction as zero, imposing no slip, and matching to $U=-Q/x$ give
$$
\boxed{f(0)=0,
\qquad f'(0)=0,
\qquad f'(\eta)\to-1\quad(\eta\to\infty)}.
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Set
$$
F(\eta)=f'(\eta)
=\frac{5-\cosh z}{1+\cosh z},
\qquad
z=\sqrt2\eta+c.
$$
Writing $C=\cosh z$, direct differentiation gives
$$
F''(\eta)=\frac{12(C-2)}{(1+C)^2}.
$$
On the other hand,
$$
1-F^2
=\frac{(1+C)^2-(5-C)^2}{(1+C)^2}
=\frac{12(C-2)}{(1+C)^2}.
$$
Thus $F''=1-F^2$, which is precisely $f'''=1-(f')^2$. The far-field condition is automatic because $F\to-1$. No slip requires
$$
0=F(0)=\frac{5-\cosh c}{1+\cosh c},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{c=\pm\operatorname{arcosh}5}.
$$
For $c=+\operatorname{arcosh}5$, $F$ decreases directly from $0$ to $-1$, matching flow everywhere toward the sink. For the negative root, $F$ initially becomes positive and creates a reverse-flow region next to the wall. The physically likely choice is therefore
$$
\boxed{c=+\operatorname{arcosh}5}.
$$

= 39B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= i
{parent=39b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <Fourier transform> convention
$$
\widehat\eta(k,t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\eta(x,t)e^{-ikx}\,dx,
\qquad
\eta(x,t)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widehat\eta(k,t)e^{ikx}\,dk.
$$
The <deep-water capillary-wave dispersion relation> makes each Fourier mode satisfy
$$
\partial_t^2\widehat\eta+S^2|k|^3\widehat\eta=0.
$$
The transformed initial data are
$$
\widehat\eta(k,0)=0,
\qquad
\partial_t\widehat\eta(k,0)
=-W\int_{-\epsilon}^{\epsilon}e^{-ikx}\,dx
=-\frac{2W\sin(k\epsilon)}k.
$$
Therefore
$$
\widehat\eta(k,t)
=-\frac{2W\sin(k\epsilon)}{kS|k|^{3/2}}
\sin(S|k|^{3/2}t).
$$
The integrand is even, so Fourier inversion gives the <surface response to a localized impulsive velocity>
$$
\boxed{
\eta(x,t)=-\frac{2W}{\pi S}
\int_0^\infty
\frac{\sin(k\epsilon)\sin(Sk^{3/2}t)\cos(kx)}{k^{5/2}}\,dk}.
$$

= ii
{parent=39b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $x=Vt$ with fixed $V>0$. The relevant <stationary point> will have $k=O(V^2/S^2)$, so the assumption $\epsilon V^2/S^2\ll1$ allows the long-wave approximation
$$
\sin(k\epsilon)\sim k\epsilon.
$$
Using $\sin A\cos B=[\sin(A+B)+\sin(A-B)]/2$, the phase $Sk^{3/2}+Vk$ has no stationary point for $k>0$. The other phase is
$$
\phi(k)=Sk^{3/2}-Vk,
$$
with
$$
\phi'(k_0)=0,
\qquad
k_0=\frac{4V^2}{9S^2},
$$
and
$$
\phi(k_0)=-\frac{4V^3}{27S^2},
\qquad
\phi''(k_0)=\frac{9S^2}{8V}>0.
$$
The stationary part of the integral is therefore
$$
-\frac{W\epsilon}{\pi S}
\int_0^\infty k^{-3/2}\sin[t\phi(k)]\,dk.
$$
By the <one-dimensional stationary-phase formula>, this is asymptotic to
$$
-\frac{W\epsilon}{\pi S}k_0^{-3/2}
\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{t\phi''(k_0)}}
\sin\left(t\phi(k_0)+\frac\pi4\right).
$$
Since
$$
k_0^{-3/2}
\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{t\phi''(k_0)}}
=\frac{9S^2\sqrt\pi}{2V^{5/2}\sqrt t},
$$
we obtain
$$
\eta(x,t)\sim
\frac{9W\epsilon S}{2\sqrt\pi V^{5/2}\sqrt t}
\sin\left(\frac{4V^3t}{27S^2}-\frac\pi4\right).
$$
Using $V=x/t$, this has the requested form
$$
\boxed{
\eta(x,t)\sim\frac{W\epsilon S t^2}{x^{5/2}}
F\!\left(\frac{x^3}{S^2t^2}\right)},
$$
where
$$
\boxed{F(q)=\frac9{2\sqrt\pi}
\sin\left(\frac{4q}{27}-\frac\pi4\right)}.
$$

= iii
{parent=39b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The stationary contribution travels with <group velocity>
$$
V=\omega'(k_0)=\frac{3S}{2}\sqrt{k_0},
$$
so $k_0=4V^2/(9S^2)$. The condition
$$
\frac{\epsilon V^2}{S^2}\ll1
$$
is therefore equivalent, up to a constant factor, to $k_0\epsilon\ll1$: the dominant <wavelength> is much longer than the impacted interval. Its Fourier transform can then be replaced by
$$
-\frac{2W\sin(k\epsilon)}k\sim-2W\epsilon,
$$
which is the total initial vertical velocity integrated over space. The far field cannot resolve $W$ and $\epsilon$ separately and depends only on their product $W\epsilon$.

= 40E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=40e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A complex square matrix $A$ is a <normal matrix> if it commutes with its <adjoint matrix>[conjugate transpose]:
$$
AA^*=A^*A.
$$
By <unitary diagonalization of a normal matrix>, there are a <unitary matrix> $U$ and a diagonal matrix $\Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_N)$ such that
$$
A=U\Lambda U^*.
$$
The <matrix 2-norm> is invariant under multiplication by unitary matrices, so
$$
\lVert A\rVert_2=\lVert\Lambda\rVert_2.
$$
For a diagonal matrix,
$$
\lVert\Lambda x\rVert_2^2
=\sum_j|\lambda_j|^2|x_j|^2
\leq\left(\max_j|\lambda_j|^2\right)\lVert x\rVert_2^2,
$$
with equality on a coordinate vector belonging to an eigenvalue of greatest modulus. Hence
$$
\boxed{\lVert A\rVert_2=\max_j|\lambda_j|=\rho(A)},
$$
the <matrix 2-norm of a normal matrix>.

= b
{parent=40e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $D\in\mathbb R^{M\times M}$ be the <Toeplitz antisymmetric tridiagonal matrix> with $D_{m,m+1}=1$, $D_{m,m-1}=-1$, and zero diagonal. Fixed homogeneous endpoint values give the homogeneous update
$$
\left(I-\frac\mu4D\right)u^{n+1}
=\left(I+\frac\mu4D\right)u^n.
$$
The hint gives the eigenvalues
$$
d_j=2i\cos\frac{j\pi}{M+1},
\qquad 1\leq j\leq M.
$$
Because each $d_j$ is purely imaginary, $1-(\mu/4)d_j\ne0$ for every real $\mu$, so the left matrix is invertible. The two matrices are polynomials in $D$ and therefore have the same orthonormal eigenvectors. The amplification matrix
$$
Q=\left(I-\frac\mu4D\right)^{-1}
\left(I+\frac\mu4D\right)
$$
has eigenvalues
$$
q_j=
\frac{1+i(\mu/2)\cos(j\pi/(M+1))}
{1-i(\mu/2)\cos(j\pi/(M+1))}.
$$
The numerator and denominator are complex conjugates, so $|q_j|=1$. Moreover, the common orthonormal eigenbasis makes $Q$ a <normal matrix>. By the <matrix 2-norm of a normal matrix>,
$$
\lVert Q^n\rVert_2=\rho(Q^n)=1
$$
for every $n$. Thus the <Crank-Nicolson centered-advection scheme on a finite interval> is unconditionally stable:
$$
\boxed{\text{every }\mu>0.}
$$

= c
{parent=40e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Insert the Fourier mode
$$
u_m^n=\zeta^n e^{im\theta}
$$
into the <leapfrog advection scheme>. This gives
$$
\zeta^{n+1}
=\mu(e^{i\theta}-e^{-i\theta})\zeta^n+\zeta^{n-1},
$$
and hence the amplification polynomial
$$
\zeta^2-2i\mu\sin\theta\,\zeta-1=0.
$$
Its roots are
$$
\zeta_\pm=i\mu\sin\theta
\pm\sqrt{1-\mu^2\sin^2\theta}.
$$
If $0<\mu<1$, the square root is real for every $\theta$, and
$$
|\zeta_\pm|^2
=\mu^2\sin^2\theta+1-\mu^2\sin^2\theta=1.
$$
If $\mu>1$, choosing $|\sin\theta|>1/\mu$ makes the roots non-real multiples of $i$ with reciprocal moduli, one of which exceeds one, so the method is unstable.

The endpoint $\mu=1$ requires care. At $\theta=\pi/2$ the polynomial is
$$
(\zeta-i)^2,
$$
so a generic mode has the form $(c_1+c_2n)i^n$ and grows linearly. It satisfies the weaker test $|\zeta_\pm|\leq1$ often quoted in elementary Fourier analysis, but it violates the <root condition for a multistep method> because the unit-modulus root is repeated. Therefore the rigorous uniform stability range for arbitrary two-level initial data is
$$
\boxed{0<\mu<1}.
$$
If “stable” is being used only for the non-amplification condition on root moduli, the conventionally quoted range is $0<\mu\leq1$, with $\mu=1$ a defective marginal endpoint rather than a uniformly stable case.
